1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,480

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:08,920
I decided to cover both open
problems in deep reinforcement

3
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:12,900
learning and how to
do deep RL research.

4
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:16,280
So let's start with part 1 and
talking about open problems

5
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:19,640
and frontiers in deep
reinforcement learning.

6
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:22,040
I'm going to break this into
a few different categories.

7
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:25,840
The first is challenges on
defining the problem setup.

8
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:28,100
The second is
challenges in methods.

9
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:32,960
And the third is challenges
in deployment and evaluation.

10
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:35,740
I should also mention that
this will not be exhaustive.

11
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:40,160
There's many problems
and many open challenges

12
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:41,860
in deep reinforcement learning.

13
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:44,960
I'm just picking out some of the
ones that I think I've either

14
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:47,960
gotten questions about quite
a bit or some of the problems

15
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:50,240
that I think are
the most exciting

16
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:54,160
and the most interesting.

17
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:55,280
So let's dive in.

18
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:58,720
And in particular,
in terms of some

19
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:02,130
of the challenges in how we
set up the deep RL problem,

20
00:01:02,130 --> 00:01:04,690
we've seen a lot of
successes in domains

21
00:01:04,690 --> 00:01:07,410
like games, math, reasoning,
coding problems, where you can

22
00:01:07,410 --> 00:01:09,430
very easily define the reward.

23
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:12,290
You can very easily
verify whether or not

24
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:15,610
the reinforcement learning
system did the correct thing,

25
00:01:15,610 --> 00:01:17,290
which is great.

26
00:01:17,290 --> 00:01:21,690
But in many real-world
scenarios, rewards don't exist.

27
00:01:21,690 --> 00:01:25,090
Or rewards are very delayed.

28
00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:30,450
And this introduces challenges
in the typical problem setting.

29
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:33,410
And so, for example, let's
think about language models

30
00:01:33,410 --> 00:01:35,850
for chatbots.

31
00:01:35,850 --> 00:01:38,950
Right now, we have
preference optimization.

32
00:01:38,950 --> 00:01:41,370
And one might actually say
that preference optimization

33
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:43,970
for chatbots seems
to work quite well.

34
00:01:43,970 --> 00:01:47,120
We're able to actually deploy
reinforcement learning at scale.

35
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:50,810
But there's actually
pretty big problems

36
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:52,450
with preference optimization.

37
00:01:52,450 --> 00:01:54,930
And as one example, there
was this research paper

38
00:01:54,930 --> 00:02:00,590
by Sharma and Tong that
was evaluating what

39
00:02:00,590 --> 00:02:02,270
preferences people are giving.

40
00:02:02,270 --> 00:02:06,190
And the x-axis is
the probability

41
00:02:06,190 --> 00:02:07,830
that a response with
the given feature

42
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:11,030
is preferred when
all else is equal.

43
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:15,910
And there's different things
here on the left axis.

44
00:02:15,910 --> 00:02:19,310
And the things that
were most preferred

45
00:02:19,310 --> 00:02:24,310
in the human preferences
were whether it was something

46
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:27,590
that the human agreed with and
whether or not the response

47
00:02:27,590 --> 00:02:29,248
was confident.

48
00:02:29,248 --> 00:02:31,790
So essentially, the things that
people are giving preferences

49
00:02:31,790 --> 00:02:35,350
for are whether or not the
language model response

50
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:38,205
is something that's
confidently saying something

51
00:02:38,205 --> 00:02:39,830
and whether that
thing that it's saying

52
00:02:39,830 --> 00:02:42,510
agrees with what
the person thinks.

53
00:02:42,510 --> 00:02:45,610
And then all the way
down here is factuality.

54
00:02:45,610 --> 00:02:47,110
And so they're
actually caring about

55
00:02:47,110 --> 00:02:49,750
whether it's confident more than
whether or not the response is

56
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:53,230
actually correct or true.

57
00:02:53,230 --> 00:02:56,000
And so people aren't actually
giving good preferences.

58
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,840
Arguably, I think we
would want language models

59
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:03,000
to be valuing factuality
over things like confidence.

60
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,520
And we're more optimizing
for what people kind of want

61
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:12,360
to hear rather than the kinds
of responses that will be good.

62
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:14,640
Now, if you also think about
preference optimization

63
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:17,120
for personalization,
for example,

64
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:20,692
we might want to develop
language models that

65
00:03:20,692 --> 00:03:22,400
are telling people
what they want to hear

66
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:23,780
and the kinds of
recommendations,

67
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:27,600
for example, that are closely
personalized to their interests

68
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:29,200
and their beliefs.

69
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:31,160
But on the flip side,
if we personalize

70
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:33,720
these kinds of models
for the many people that

71
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:35,520
use these models,
then it might lead

72
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:38,720
to polarization in people
who, for example, have

73
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:41,440
extreme beliefs rather
than being exposed

74
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:43,100
to a wide range of beliefs.

75
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:45,240
And so even something
like personalization

76
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:48,823
may not intrinsically be
what we want to optimize for.

77
00:03:48,823 --> 00:03:50,240
And at the end of
the day, there's

78
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:53,620
typically multiple things that
we do care about optimizing for.

79
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:56,140
And balancing those different
objectives that may actually

80
00:03:56,140 --> 00:03:58,420
compete with each
other ends up being

81
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:01,500
a very challenging problem.

82
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:03,060
So this whole kind
of realm of how

83
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:06,300
do we define rewards and
the kind of objective

84
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:10,100
as a whole for
language models is--

85
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:11,840
in many ways, there's
a lot of progress.

86
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:13,790
But it's still a really
challenging problem.

87
00:04:13,790 --> 00:04:16,500

88
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:20,410
Now, separate from language
models, there's also robotics.

89
00:04:20,410 --> 00:04:22,740
Right now, in
robotics, either you

90
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:25,260
have binary rewards
in the real world

91
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:28,300
or, if in simulation, you
might have hand-shaped rewards,

92
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:30,860
where you define different
terms for what you want.

93
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:34,400
This takes a lot of effort, ends
up being fairly challenging,

94
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:38,020
or takes a lot of
labeling annotations.

95
00:04:38,020 --> 00:04:40,820
And even then, even when
you have human annotations,

96
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:44,080
it can be tricky
to judge rewards.

97
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:47,340
And so, for example, if you were
to look at these shirts that

98
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:52,310
were each folded by a robot, if
you were to think about which

99
00:04:52,310 --> 00:04:57,470
shirt was folded the best or try
to give rewards for these shirt

100
00:04:57,470 --> 00:04:59,430
folds, it's rather
tricky to figure out

101
00:04:59,430 --> 00:05:02,390
how you might reward different
mistakes, different amounts

102
00:05:02,390 --> 00:05:07,110
of crinkleness, whether or not
the shirt is off center or not.

103
00:05:07,110 --> 00:05:10,230
It's generally a very ambiguous
problem and hard even for people

104
00:05:10,230 --> 00:05:12,820
to think about how to provide
rewards for these systems.

105
00:05:12,820 --> 00:05:16,430

106
00:05:16,430 --> 00:05:20,470
Another example-- let's think
about YouTube recommendations.

107
00:05:20,470 --> 00:05:22,550
What I mean here is if
when you're on YouTube,

108
00:05:22,550 --> 00:05:24,630
there's recommendations
for a next video

109
00:05:24,630 --> 00:05:27,510
to watch after watching
your current video.

110
00:05:27,510 --> 00:05:34,510
And these an
algorithm that selects

111
00:05:34,510 --> 00:05:37,510
what these
recommendations should be.

112
00:05:37,510 --> 00:05:40,610
Now, we don't have exact details
on how this algorithm works.

113
00:05:40,610 --> 00:05:43,670
But there was a
research paper that

114
00:05:43,670 --> 00:05:46,710
actually was talking
a little bit about how

115
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:48,870
they do these recommendations.

116
00:05:48,870 --> 00:05:52,030
And as you might expect, when
they do these recommendations,

117
00:05:52,030 --> 00:05:53,572
they want to optimize
for engagement,

118
00:05:53,572 --> 00:05:55,280
which is like whether
or not you actually

119
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:56,690
clicked on the recommendation.

120
00:05:56,690 --> 00:05:58,398
And if you clicked on
the recommendation,

121
00:05:58,398 --> 00:06:00,323
that was probably a
good recommendation.

122
00:06:00,323 --> 00:06:02,990
And also, satisfaction, which is
that after you clicked on that,

123
00:06:02,990 --> 00:06:05,250
if you actually like
the video, then that's

124
00:06:05,250 --> 00:06:08,050
also a good sign that it
was a good recommendation.

125
00:06:08,050 --> 00:06:10,850
So this all seems reasonable.

126
00:06:10,850 --> 00:06:14,050
But another thing that was
mentioned in the research paper

127
00:06:14,050 --> 00:06:18,290
was that the combination of
these metrics for the reward

128
00:06:18,290 --> 00:06:20,650
function was actually
manually tuned

129
00:06:20,650 --> 00:06:23,770
by a person rather
than something

130
00:06:23,770 --> 00:06:26,290
that is designed automatically.

131
00:06:26,290 --> 00:06:29,890
This kind of also goes
to show the challenge

132
00:06:29,890 --> 00:06:32,370
in actually defining these
kinds of reward functions

133
00:06:32,370 --> 00:06:34,690
when we don't know
how best to balance

134
00:06:34,690 --> 00:06:36,110
these kinds of objectives.

135
00:06:36,110 --> 00:06:40,650

136
00:06:40,650 --> 00:06:42,230
We talked a bit
about math reasoning.

137
00:06:42,230 --> 00:06:43,650
If we want to take
those advances

138
00:06:43,650 --> 00:06:47,450
and apply them to broader
scientific experimentation

139
00:06:47,450 --> 00:06:49,340
and reasoning, you
often don't necessarily

140
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:52,500
know what the final
answer should be.

141
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:55,240
And so even training
on math reasoning,

142
00:06:55,240 --> 00:06:57,558
you might see some transfer
to scientific reasoning.

143
00:06:57,558 --> 00:06:59,100
But if you want to
train on a broader

144
00:06:59,100 --> 00:07:01,460
set of scientific
reasoning problems,

145
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:05,140
this sort of verification
becomes challenging.

146
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:06,980
And the last thing
I'll mention along

147
00:07:06,980 --> 00:07:09,700
these sorts of rewards
and verification

148
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:13,020
is that if we think
about how humans learn,

149
00:07:13,020 --> 00:07:16,220
humans often learn by
rolling around on the floor,

150
00:07:16,220 --> 00:07:18,180
playing with random toys.

151
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:22,100
And it seems like they're
not just optimizing

152
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:23,820
for one particular task.

153
00:07:23,820 --> 00:07:25,460
They're optimizing--
well, who knows

154
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:28,833
what they're optimizing for?

155
00:07:28,833 --> 00:07:30,500
For example, we could
develop algorithms

156
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:32,980
that would allow machines
to explore in this way,

157
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:34,980
potentially optimizing
for learning, potentially

158
00:07:34,980 --> 00:07:36,940
optimizing for exploration.

159
00:07:36,940 --> 00:07:41,500
And this is certainly
an open problem.

160
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:44,700
So that's on the problem setup.

161
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:46,880
Now, let's talk a little
bit about challenges

162
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:49,160
on the method side.

163
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:53,240
So the first topic
I wanted to bring up

164
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:56,040
was leveraging prior
data or knowledge.

165
00:07:56,040 --> 00:07:58,000
So there's a lot
of circumstances

166
00:07:58,000 --> 00:07:59,703
where we're not
starting reinforcement

167
00:07:59,703 --> 00:08:00,620
learning from scratch.

168
00:08:00,620 --> 00:08:03,480
And we have some prior
data, some prior knowledge

169
00:08:03,480 --> 00:08:05,600
about what is best to explore.

170
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:08,520
And the default tool for
leveraging previous knowledge

171
00:08:08,520 --> 00:08:11,100
and data is to use
pre-trained model weights

172
00:08:11,100 --> 00:08:14,160
or to initialize your replay
buffer with any previous data

173
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:16,800
that you have.

174
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:20,280
But this tool doesn't extend
to all sorts of scenarios

175
00:08:20,280 --> 00:08:21,657
we might care about.

176
00:08:21,657 --> 00:08:23,240
There might be
scenarios where we have

177
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:25,180
more abstract prior knowledge.

178
00:08:25,180 --> 00:08:26,680
Maybe we have some
knowledge that we

179
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:28,720
read from news articles
that isn't kind

180
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:30,500
of in the typical data format.

181
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:33,820
We want to incorporate
those facts into our model.

182
00:08:33,820 --> 00:08:36,320
Maybe we have some
hints that could be

183
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:40,120
helpful for solving a problem.

184
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:42,320
And even separate
from that, if we

185
00:08:42,320 --> 00:08:45,010
think about this
kind of paradigm

186
00:08:45,010 --> 00:08:48,255
of using pre-training and
initialized data sets,

187
00:08:48,255 --> 00:08:50,130
there are scenarios
where this might actually

188
00:08:50,130 --> 00:08:52,410
be too constraining.

189
00:08:52,410 --> 00:08:56,590
And so as one example, here's
a research paper by Ball et al.

190
00:08:56,590 --> 00:08:58,330
In 2023.

191
00:08:58,330 --> 00:08:59,770
And they introduced
a method that

192
00:08:59,770 --> 00:09:02,410
was studying using
prior demonstration data

193
00:09:02,410 --> 00:09:04,370
with reinforcement learning.

194
00:09:04,370 --> 00:09:07,170
And the method that
they introduced,

195
00:09:07,170 --> 00:09:09,390
they initialized the buffer.

196
00:09:09,390 --> 00:09:11,650
But they actually didn't
pre-train the weights.

197
00:09:11,650 --> 00:09:13,483
And they found that
pre-training the weights

198
00:09:13,483 --> 00:09:17,050
did worse than not
pre-training the weights.

199
00:09:17,050 --> 00:09:19,450
And so this kind of
suggests that there

200
00:09:19,450 --> 00:09:22,770
might be scenarios where
using pre-trained weights

201
00:09:22,770 --> 00:09:24,970
or using pre-trained data
sets might actually be

202
00:09:24,970 --> 00:09:27,970
too constraining on our model.

203
00:09:27,970 --> 00:09:32,690
As one example of this, if
you have a pre-trained model,

204
00:09:32,690 --> 00:09:34,850
the output layer of
that pre-trained model

205
00:09:34,850 --> 00:09:37,210
might have converged to
something fairly deterministic.

206
00:09:37,210 --> 00:09:39,210
And then if you're trying
to learn something new

207
00:09:39,210 --> 00:09:44,030
or adapt it in a new
way, then that last layer

208
00:09:44,030 --> 00:09:47,470
might not actually be
exploring in a good way.

209
00:09:47,470 --> 00:09:51,590
So that's one example of why
you might see worse performance

210
00:09:51,590 --> 00:09:56,070
if you initialize your weights
with some previous neural

211
00:09:56,070 --> 00:09:57,590
network.

212
00:09:57,590 --> 00:09:59,670
And arguably, in
language models,

213
00:09:59,670 --> 00:10:02,310
perhaps reinforcement
learning works quite well

214
00:10:02,310 --> 00:10:05,670
because pre-trained models often
have a very diverse distribution

215
00:10:05,670 --> 00:10:08,630
over output tokens.

216
00:10:08,630 --> 00:10:12,270
And in terms of leveraging
prior data and knowledge

217
00:10:12,270 --> 00:10:14,050
and also being able
to go beyond it,

218
00:10:14,050 --> 00:10:16,508
I think that this question is
really important for thinking

219
00:10:16,508 --> 00:10:19,070
about how language models can
solve problems that humans

220
00:10:19,070 --> 00:10:21,310
haven't solved
because they probably

221
00:10:21,310 --> 00:10:24,050
should build upon the knowledge
in the pre-trained data sets.

222
00:10:24,050 --> 00:10:26,390
But they also need to think
about how to go beyond that

223
00:10:26,390 --> 00:10:29,510
and not be too constrained by
what the pre-trained model might

224
00:10:29,510 --> 00:10:31,270
say.

225
00:10:31,270 --> 00:10:34,070
And it's also quite
useful for thinking

226
00:10:34,070 --> 00:10:36,950
about how robots or
autonomous vehicles might

227
00:10:36,950 --> 00:10:40,190
learn how to do things
faster and more reliably

228
00:10:40,190 --> 00:10:41,660
than what humans do.

229
00:10:41,660 --> 00:10:44,360
And perhaps if we
constrain too much

230
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:47,760
to the pre-trained knowledge
or the prior knowledge,

231
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:50,400
they might not be able to
surpass human abilities in terms

232
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:53,370
of speed or reliability.

233
00:10:53,370 --> 00:10:55,120
So I think, generally,
these two questions

234
00:10:55,120 --> 00:10:56,840
are really fascinating.

235
00:10:56,840 --> 00:11:00,880
And we've seen some suggestions
that reinforcement learning

236
00:11:00,880 --> 00:11:01,760
can do this.

237
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:04,320
And it's certainly one of the
most promising tools for going

238
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:07,040
beyond what humans can do.

239
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:12,080
But lots of interesting
and open work to do there.

240
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:14,360
Now, one question I've
gotten quite a bit

241
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:17,640
is whether we can leverage world
models and video generation

242
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:21,560
models for robotics and control.

243
00:11:21,560 --> 00:11:24,840
And we've seen some really
impressive results, especially

244
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:27,340
recently from video
generation models,

245
00:11:27,340 --> 00:11:30,070
where they clearly have a lot of
rich knowledge about the world,

246
00:11:30,070 --> 00:11:34,680
like how cooking on a stove
works, how typing on a keyboard

247
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:38,640
works, and how that might look
like if the keyboard was made

248
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:43,260
of gummy bears, and also
how things like even walking

249
00:11:43,260 --> 00:11:45,595
at an abstract level work.

250
00:11:45,595 --> 00:11:47,220
So it seems like this
sort of knowledge

251
00:11:47,220 --> 00:11:49,190
should be really useful
for learning behavior.

252
00:11:49,190 --> 00:11:51,780

253
00:11:51,780 --> 00:11:53,620
But there's actually
a lot of nuance

254
00:11:53,620 --> 00:11:57,900
and a lot of challenges with
using these kinds of models.

255
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:02,780
As an example, if you train
an action-conditioned video

256
00:12:02,780 --> 00:12:04,240
generation model--

257
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:07,140
so a model that takes as
input the current image

258
00:12:07,140 --> 00:12:11,100
and future actions and
predicts future video--

259
00:12:11,100 --> 00:12:14,420
and if you train that on
demonstrations and rollouts

260
00:12:14,420 --> 00:12:19,700
from one policy and then
you evaluate a new policy's

261
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:23,980
actions with that model and
evaluate whether or not that

262
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:27,180
those actions lead
to good outcomes,

263
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:29,940
then these actions
won't necessarily

264
00:12:29,940 --> 00:12:34,440
be represented in the data
that the model was trained on.

265
00:12:34,440 --> 00:12:38,070
And as a result, the model often
will make inaccurate predictions

266
00:12:38,070 --> 00:12:40,230
for these actions.

267
00:12:40,230 --> 00:12:42,210
If most of the data
set is demonstrations,

268
00:12:42,210 --> 00:12:43,670
then it will
predict a video that

269
00:12:43,670 --> 00:12:45,730
corresponds to
correctly doing the task

270
00:12:45,730 --> 00:12:48,950
even if these actions did not
lead to a correct outcome.

271
00:12:48,950 --> 00:12:51,750
And generally, these models
have very little data--

272
00:12:51,750 --> 00:12:54,750
for example, like
narrowly making a mistake

273
00:12:54,750 --> 00:12:57,270
or making mistakes that are
very different from the kinds

274
00:12:57,270 --> 00:13:00,710
of mistakes that humans make.

275
00:13:00,710 --> 00:13:03,550
The other thing here is that
even small physical inaccuracies

276
00:13:03,550 --> 00:13:05,990
can lead to poor performance.

277
00:13:05,990 --> 00:13:09,547
And so a lot of times, these
videos look really, really good.

278
00:13:09,547 --> 00:13:11,130
They look like they
might be accurate.

279
00:13:11,130 --> 00:13:14,630
But that doesn't mean that the
underlying physics is actually

280
00:13:14,630 --> 00:13:16,270
accurate.

281
00:13:16,270 --> 00:13:18,270
Now, I think that there
are solutions here.

282
00:13:18,270 --> 00:13:20,350
One possible solution
is to try to train

283
00:13:20,350 --> 00:13:22,470
on data from lots of
different policies

284
00:13:22,470 --> 00:13:24,550
with this kind of
approach, with the hopes

285
00:13:24,550 --> 00:13:28,190
that that will lead to
enough coverage over action

286
00:13:28,190 --> 00:13:32,150
distributions to
leverage these models.

287
00:13:32,150 --> 00:13:33,750
And then the second
possible solution

288
00:13:33,750 --> 00:13:36,450
is that we might be able to
use these video generation

289
00:13:36,450 --> 00:13:39,170
models in other ways.

290
00:13:39,170 --> 00:13:42,010
And one example of
that, maybe instead

291
00:13:42,010 --> 00:13:46,090
of training this sort of
physical model of the future,

292
00:13:46,090 --> 00:13:51,090
we could generate,
for example, what

293
00:13:51,090 --> 00:13:55,450
should the future video look
like not conditioned on actions?

294
00:13:55,450 --> 00:13:58,370
And if we train this
only on demonstrations,

295
00:13:58,370 --> 00:14:02,330
then, for example, we could
predict future video and plan

296
00:14:02,330 --> 00:14:06,930
out how to accomplish the task
in the future in video space

297
00:14:06,930 --> 00:14:10,090
and then run a goal
conditioned policy

298
00:14:10,090 --> 00:14:14,170
using the predicted video.

299
00:14:14,170 --> 00:14:16,690
And something like this kind
of sidesteps the challenge

300
00:14:16,690 --> 00:14:18,610
of this sort of
out-of-distribution action

301
00:14:18,610 --> 00:14:20,130
problem because
you're only using

302
00:14:20,130 --> 00:14:23,770
the model for a form of
planning where you're thinking

303
00:14:23,770 --> 00:14:26,170
through what is a good
outcome in the future

304
00:14:26,170 --> 00:14:27,690
rather than thinking
through what

305
00:14:27,690 --> 00:14:29,300
is the outcome of
specific actions.

306
00:14:29,300 --> 00:14:34,440

307
00:14:34,440 --> 00:14:36,633
And then the third thing
on the method side--

308
00:14:36,633 --> 00:14:38,300
the third challenge
that I'll talk about

309
00:14:38,300 --> 00:14:41,020
is thinking about scaling.

310
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:46,900
So on the scaling front,
we've seen large-scale RL

311
00:14:46,900 --> 00:14:49,040
succeed with language models.

312
00:14:49,040 --> 00:14:52,420
And that's actually
really exciting to see.

313
00:14:52,420 --> 00:14:54,780
But there's still
shortcomings there.

314
00:14:54,780 --> 00:14:58,660
So a lot of language
model RL is considering

315
00:14:58,660 --> 00:15:02,860
problems that are still fairly
short horizon or extremely

316
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:06,180
online, like generating
rollouts of the model

317
00:15:06,180 --> 00:15:09,620
every single iteration.

318
00:15:09,620 --> 00:15:12,060
And as an example
of this, if we're

319
00:15:12,060 --> 00:15:14,540
doing preference
optimization, typically we

320
00:15:14,540 --> 00:15:16,860
consider this
single turn dialogue

321
00:15:16,860 --> 00:15:19,060
setting, where we're
getting rewards just

322
00:15:19,060 --> 00:15:21,700
for our next
response rather than

323
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:25,980
trying to reward the outcome
of the full conversation.

324
00:15:25,980 --> 00:15:28,692
And this means that it's
a shorter horizon problem.

325
00:15:28,692 --> 00:15:31,150
It means you don't have to have
a human in the loop of data

326
00:15:31,150 --> 00:15:34,990
collection to generate those
dialogue conversations.

327
00:15:34,990 --> 00:15:37,870
And so this makes
the problem easier,

328
00:15:37,870 --> 00:15:39,767
which is good to make
the problem easier.

329
00:15:39,767 --> 00:15:42,350
But it means that if there are
scenarios where you have longer

330
00:15:42,350 --> 00:15:44,990
horizon problems or you need
a human in the loop of data

331
00:15:44,990 --> 00:15:48,150
collection, that becomes a
lot more challenging to do

332
00:15:48,150 --> 00:15:51,270
at large scale.

333
00:15:51,270 --> 00:15:53,550
As another example, if
we think about reasoning

334
00:15:53,550 --> 00:15:56,830
for math problems, this
relies on a very large number

335
00:15:56,830 --> 00:16:02,870
of online samples from the model
interleaved with policy updates.

336
00:16:02,870 --> 00:16:05,510
And there are some scenarios
where it's actually really hard

337
00:16:05,510 --> 00:16:09,650
to collect that very online data
interleaved with policy updates,

338
00:16:09,650 --> 00:16:12,830
especially if collecting data
involves interfacing with humans

339
00:16:12,830 --> 00:16:14,920
or interfacing with
the physical world.

340
00:16:14,920 --> 00:16:17,870

341
00:16:17,870 --> 00:16:21,390
So towards this
end, if we wanted

342
00:16:21,390 --> 00:16:24,230
to think about longer
horizon problems

343
00:16:24,230 --> 00:16:26,650
and efficiently learning,
tools like value functions

344
00:16:26,650 --> 00:16:27,650
should be really useful.

345
00:16:27,650 --> 00:16:31,570

346
00:16:31,570 --> 00:16:34,010
We've seen some signs of life
in training value functions

347
00:16:34,010 --> 00:16:34,750
at large scale.

348
00:16:34,750 --> 00:16:38,970
And when using PPO, we train
value functions at large scale.

349
00:16:38,970 --> 00:16:41,370
But these value
functions are primarily

350
00:16:41,370 --> 00:16:43,350
used for reducing
gradient variants

351
00:16:43,350 --> 00:16:45,850
and may not be accurate
enough for doing

352
00:16:45,850 --> 00:16:48,770
a full actor-critic update
or for selecting actions

353
00:16:48,770 --> 00:16:51,690
with a Q-learning
style objective.

354
00:16:51,690 --> 00:16:55,290
And so understanding whether
we can scale that value

355
00:16:55,290 --> 00:16:57,730
function learning and
have value functions

356
00:16:57,730 --> 00:17:00,910
be accurate enough for directly
optimizing policies-- something

357
00:17:00,910 --> 00:17:02,410
that's pretty
interesting to explore

358
00:17:02,410 --> 00:17:05,320
and might be important for
longer horizon problems.

359
00:17:05,320 --> 00:17:08,530

360
00:17:08,530 --> 00:17:13,609
And then second, I mentioned
that very online data collection

361
00:17:13,609 --> 00:17:17,170
can be challenging
at large scale.

362
00:17:17,170 --> 00:17:22,170
And as an example of
this, say that you are--

363
00:17:22,170 --> 00:17:26,250

364
00:17:26,250 --> 00:17:28,780
maybe say that you're like an
autonomous driving company.

365
00:17:28,780 --> 00:17:31,060
You want to do RL.

366
00:17:31,060 --> 00:17:34,740
It's going to be hard to
collect data on all of your cars

367
00:17:34,740 --> 00:17:41,140
at once, then do a policy
update every 30 seconds or even

368
00:17:41,140 --> 00:17:43,060
every minute or even every hour.

369
00:17:43,060 --> 00:17:46,100
It's much easier to first
collect a large batch of data,

370
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:49,420
like maybe over a couple
of days, maybe over a week,

371
00:17:49,420 --> 00:17:51,780
then update your
policy on all that data

372
00:17:51,780 --> 00:17:53,900
and repeat that process.

373
00:17:53,900 --> 00:17:57,035
And so I refer to that sort of
problem statement as a batch

374
00:17:57,035 --> 00:17:58,660
online setting, where
you're collecting

375
00:17:58,660 --> 00:18:02,380
a batch of online data with one
policy, then doing an update

376
00:18:02,380 --> 00:18:04,600
and repeating.

377
00:18:04,600 --> 00:18:07,880
This more batch data
collection setting

378
00:18:07,880 --> 00:18:09,380
might have challenges
that are a bit

379
00:18:09,380 --> 00:18:13,190
different from your typical
online reinforcement learning

380
00:18:13,190 --> 00:18:13,690
setting.

381
00:18:13,690 --> 00:18:16,900

382
00:18:16,900 --> 00:18:19,877
And so this sort
of data collection

383
00:18:19,877 --> 00:18:20,960
should be quite practical.

384
00:18:20,960 --> 00:18:23,043
You could also do this
potentially asynchronously,

385
00:18:23,043 --> 00:18:25,100
where you're collecting
data asynchronously

386
00:18:25,100 --> 00:18:26,840
from making updates.

387
00:18:26,840 --> 00:18:30,160
And at large scale, this
sort of asynchronous updates

388
00:18:30,160 --> 00:18:34,633
can actually potentially
also be more practical.

389
00:18:34,633 --> 00:18:36,800
And there may be new
considerations on the algorithm

390
00:18:36,800 --> 00:18:37,720
side.

391
00:18:37,720 --> 00:18:40,380
So for example, in one
recent work by Dong et al.,

392
00:18:40,380 --> 00:18:42,520
they found that using
more expressive policies.

393
00:18:42,520 --> 00:18:44,880
Like, diffusion policies
was really, really important

394
00:18:44,880 --> 00:18:46,600
for effective
reinforcement learning

395
00:18:46,600 --> 00:18:48,353
in this batch online setting.

396
00:18:48,353 --> 00:18:50,520
And one explanation for
that is if you're collecting

397
00:18:50,520 --> 00:18:51,820
a large batch of data.

398
00:18:51,820 --> 00:18:55,080
Having diverse
actions be represented

399
00:18:55,080 --> 00:18:57,880
could naturally be
pretty important.

400
00:18:57,880 --> 00:19:00,220
Because in the fully
online setting,

401
00:19:00,220 --> 00:19:02,235
if you have a more
deterministic policy,

402
00:19:02,235 --> 00:19:03,860
if you're going to
be making an update,

403
00:19:03,860 --> 00:19:06,760
maybe it's OK to be more
deterministic because you're

404
00:19:06,760 --> 00:19:09,168
going to change your policy
really, really soon anyway.

405
00:19:09,168 --> 00:19:10,960
And so you'll already
get more diverse data

406
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:13,640
just from changing your
policy so frequently.

407
00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:15,720
Whereas in the
more batch setting,

408
00:19:15,720 --> 00:19:18,200
you don't have the luxury
of changing your policy so

409
00:19:18,200 --> 00:19:18,880
frequently.

410
00:19:18,880 --> 00:19:21,760
And having a more
diverse distribution

411
00:19:21,760 --> 00:19:24,420
might be quite helpful.

412
00:19:24,420 --> 00:19:27,730
So DPO-- there are algorithms
that are operating much more

413
00:19:27,730 --> 00:19:29,170
offline.

414
00:19:29,170 --> 00:19:31,410
And so there is success there.

415
00:19:31,410 --> 00:19:33,210
The one downside of
that is those problems

416
00:19:33,210 --> 00:19:34,810
are shorter horizon.

417
00:19:34,810 --> 00:19:38,250
And so can we do long horizons
and with less online data?

418
00:19:38,250 --> 00:19:40,090
So yeah, things like
offline RL are also

419
00:19:40,090 --> 00:19:43,930
going to be more
scalable as well.

420
00:19:43,930 --> 00:19:46,230
Let's talk a bit about
deployment and evaluation.

421
00:19:46,230 --> 00:19:52,530
So one problem I think
that is quite important

422
00:19:52,530 --> 00:19:54,450
with these models is
thinking about safety

423
00:19:54,450 --> 00:19:58,070
and how to apply these models
in more safety critical domains.

424
00:19:58,070 --> 00:20:00,730
That could be domains like
medicine, driving, mental health

425
00:20:00,730 --> 00:20:03,810
counseling, legal and
political discourse, and so on.

426
00:20:03,810 --> 00:20:06,690
No shortage of domains
that have challenges

427
00:20:06,690 --> 00:20:09,010
that could lead to safety
critical situations when

428
00:20:09,010 --> 00:20:11,530
you make mistakes.

429
00:20:11,530 --> 00:20:14,130
Now, historically,
this sort of problem

430
00:20:14,130 --> 00:20:16,690
has been approached by
trying to formally verify

431
00:20:16,690 --> 00:20:18,770
the behavior of the model
or provide some sort

432
00:20:18,770 --> 00:20:21,330
of probabilistic guarantees.

433
00:20:21,330 --> 00:20:24,230
But generally,
oftentimes, these methods

434
00:20:24,230 --> 00:20:27,150
are going to make assumptions
that don't hold up

435
00:20:27,150 --> 00:20:28,890
under real-world circumstances.

436
00:20:28,890 --> 00:20:32,590
And so even if you can
verify it in a model,

437
00:20:32,590 --> 00:20:38,270
those verifications often
won't hold at larger scales.

438
00:20:38,270 --> 00:20:41,350
And generally, it's impossible
to fully guarantee safety

439
00:20:41,350 --> 00:20:43,310
in the countless
number of scenarios

440
00:20:43,310 --> 00:20:46,110
that you can encounter
in the real world.

441
00:20:46,110 --> 00:20:48,710
And we find that even
humans make mistakes, even

442
00:20:48,710 --> 00:20:50,130
in safety critical settings.

443
00:20:50,130 --> 00:20:53,690
So human drivers make mistakes,
surgeons, pilots make mistakes,

444
00:20:53,690 --> 00:20:57,230
politicians make
mistakes, and so forth.

445
00:20:57,230 --> 00:20:59,530
So we can't guarantee safety.

446
00:20:59,530 --> 00:21:03,790
But we could at least try to
think about handling safety

447
00:21:03,790 --> 00:21:07,310
critical scenarios effectively.

448
00:21:07,310 --> 00:21:12,548
And arguably, if you were
to think about safety,

449
00:21:12,548 --> 00:21:14,590
there might be some
constraints that we can apply

450
00:21:14,590 --> 00:21:16,430
to the model in some scenarios.

451
00:21:16,430 --> 00:21:20,010
But in general, safety
is an open-world problem.

452
00:21:20,010 --> 00:21:22,200
You need to handle all
sorts of different ways

453
00:21:22,200 --> 00:21:26,000
in which your model could
do something that's unsafe.

454
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,440
And arguably, large-scale
machine learning

455
00:21:28,440 --> 00:21:30,760
has been the most
successful way to handle

456
00:21:30,760 --> 00:21:33,240
open-world circumstances.

457
00:21:33,240 --> 00:21:36,328
And so as a result of this,
it seems like perhaps we

458
00:21:36,328 --> 00:21:38,120
should try to collect
large amounts of data

459
00:21:38,120 --> 00:21:42,720
that would teach our model what
is safe and what is unsafe.

460
00:21:42,720 --> 00:21:45,120
Now, at the same time, this
is really unsatisfying.

461
00:21:45,120 --> 00:21:48,800
This means that we actually have
to somehow collect a lot of data

462
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:50,660
under unsafe circumstances.

463
00:21:50,660 --> 00:21:56,780
And it also can have some really
real and negative ramifications.

464
00:21:56,780 --> 00:22:00,440
So as an example,
there was news articles

465
00:22:00,440 --> 00:22:05,240
that talked about how tech
companies are basically

466
00:22:05,240 --> 00:22:10,800
trying to do content
moderation or label data that

467
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:13,880
is traumatic or toxic.

468
00:22:13,880 --> 00:22:17,160
And labeling that
kind of data can even

469
00:22:17,160 --> 00:22:20,180
cause humans all sorts of
mental health challenges

470
00:22:20,180 --> 00:22:23,900
and has a lot of
ethical considerations.

471
00:22:23,900 --> 00:22:28,100
And so this approach isn't
satisfying for handling

472
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:32,180
this sort of safety
critical problem.

473
00:22:32,180 --> 00:22:34,660
And so can we
develop methods that

474
00:22:34,660 --> 00:22:37,120
can learn what's unsafe
without expansive data,

475
00:22:37,120 --> 00:22:38,880
without expansive
human labeling,

476
00:22:38,880 --> 00:22:40,660
without expansive
data of incidents

477
00:22:40,660 --> 00:22:44,300
that happen themselves?

478
00:22:44,300 --> 00:22:46,820
Maybe we could use
synthetic data for this.

479
00:22:46,820 --> 00:22:49,300
Maybe we can use some
sort of prior knowledge.

480
00:22:49,300 --> 00:22:51,020
This is an open
challenge and would

481
00:22:51,020 --> 00:22:55,020
have a lot of benefits in terms
of the safety of these systems

482
00:22:55,020 --> 00:23:00,140
and the safety of
training these systems.

483
00:23:00,140 --> 00:23:04,360
And then separate from that,
as these models are improving,

484
00:23:04,360 --> 00:23:07,620
we also want them to remain safe
during the exploration process

485
00:23:07,620 --> 00:23:10,780
and during the learning process.

486
00:23:10,780 --> 00:23:14,140
And this is something
that humans are actually

487
00:23:14,140 --> 00:23:16,670
pretty good at doing.

488
00:23:16,670 --> 00:23:22,650
And as a silly example,
maybe a couple years ago,

489
00:23:22,650 --> 00:23:25,110
I taught myself how to ride
my bike without holding on

490
00:23:25,110 --> 00:23:26,390
to the handlebars.

491
00:23:26,390 --> 00:23:30,750
And I did so without
ever falling off my bike.

492
00:23:30,750 --> 00:23:32,130
And I'm not saying that to brag.

493
00:23:32,130 --> 00:23:35,450

494
00:23:35,450 --> 00:23:36,710
It's not that hard.

495
00:23:36,710 --> 00:23:39,507
It took time, took effort.

496
00:23:39,507 --> 00:23:41,590
But I think it's really
fascinating to think about

497
00:23:41,590 --> 00:23:44,470
how would you get a robot
to, for example, learn

498
00:23:44,470 --> 00:23:47,910
how to do a new behavior like
that without ever falling

499
00:23:47,910 --> 00:23:50,367
or without ever causing
damage to themselves.

500
00:23:50,367 --> 00:23:51,950
And it means that
you're fundamentally

501
00:23:51,950 --> 00:23:53,850
exploring a new part
of the state space

502
00:23:53,850 --> 00:23:57,070
while also understanding what
might be more risky and less

503
00:23:57,070 --> 00:24:00,870
risky and thinking about
a curriculum over how

504
00:24:00,870 --> 00:24:02,850
to accomplish that
sort of objective.

505
00:24:02,850 --> 00:24:05,376

506
00:24:05,376 --> 00:24:08,710
So separate from safety,
although related,

507
00:24:08,710 --> 00:24:13,050
is that these models do, even in
non-safety critical scenarios,

508
00:24:13,050 --> 00:24:14,350
make mistakes.

509
00:24:14,350 --> 00:24:18,147
Sometimes, these mistakes
are called hallucinations.

510
00:24:18,147 --> 00:24:19,730
Probably, many of
you have encountered

511
00:24:19,730 --> 00:24:21,690
these kinds of mistakes before.

512
00:24:21,690 --> 00:24:23,650
I'll provide one
example here, where

513
00:24:23,650 --> 00:24:27,410
I was asking it to tell me
what the weight of the sugar

514
00:24:27,410 --> 00:24:29,707
on the bowl is in this image.

515
00:24:29,707 --> 00:24:32,290
And it confidently told me that
the digital scale on the image

516
00:24:32,290 --> 00:24:34,450
reads 108.2 grams.

517
00:24:34,450 --> 00:24:36,770
That is the weight of the
sugar currently in the bowl.

518
00:24:36,770 --> 00:24:38,217
This answer is incorrect.

519
00:24:38,217 --> 00:24:40,550
It's actually a bit different
from what the scale shows,

520
00:24:40,550 --> 00:24:41,970
although it's also
very challenging

521
00:24:41,970 --> 00:24:43,470
to read what the
scale shows because

522
00:24:43,470 --> 00:24:44,820
of the glare in the image.

523
00:24:44,820 --> 00:24:47,410

524
00:24:47,410 --> 00:24:51,770
And oftentimes, in
language models,

525
00:24:51,770 --> 00:24:56,690
humans are the recipient of the
predictions of these models.

526
00:24:56,690 --> 00:24:58,850
And this means that
oftentimes mistakes

527
00:24:58,850 --> 00:25:02,650
are fairly manageable because a
human can discern whether or not

528
00:25:02,650 --> 00:25:05,430
the model was saying the right
thing or the wrong thing.

529
00:25:05,430 --> 00:25:08,090

530
00:25:08,090 --> 00:25:10,410
But the interface between
these humans and these models

531
00:25:10,410 --> 00:25:13,100
is not well optimized.

532
00:25:13,100 --> 00:25:18,580
And as an example of this,
there were multiple studies

533
00:25:18,580 --> 00:25:23,380
that have looked at how AI
systems work with doctors.

534
00:25:23,380 --> 00:25:26,220
And in one study,
it found that when

535
00:25:26,220 --> 00:25:28,260
the AI was working
independently,

536
00:25:28,260 --> 00:25:31,980
it was able to
achieve 92% accuracy.

537
00:25:31,980 --> 00:25:34,860
When physicians were
using AI assistants,

538
00:25:34,860 --> 00:25:38,420
they only had 76% accuracy.

539
00:25:38,420 --> 00:25:41,480
And that was barely better than
when humans were acting alone,

540
00:25:41,480 --> 00:25:44,220
which was 74% accuracy.

541
00:25:44,220 --> 00:25:46,840
And it seems like for these
systems to be more effective,

542
00:25:46,840 --> 00:25:48,940
we should take into account
the fact that they'll

543
00:25:48,940 --> 00:25:52,900
be working with humans with
actually making decisions

544
00:25:52,900 --> 00:25:56,180
at the end of the day.

545
00:25:56,180 --> 00:25:58,713
Like, 92 sounds
great compared to 74.

546
00:25:58,713 --> 00:26:00,380
But if it is going
to be a system that's

547
00:26:00,380 --> 00:26:04,010
interacting with a person, 76
doesn't sound quite so great.

548
00:26:04,010 --> 00:26:06,940

549
00:26:06,940 --> 00:26:10,020
So can we optimize for
better human AI systems?

550
00:26:10,020 --> 00:26:13,000
And as part of this, maybe
one way to achieve this

551
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:16,383
was if models could better
estimate uncertainty and better

552
00:26:16,383 --> 00:26:17,800
estimate when they
might be giving

553
00:26:17,800 --> 00:26:19,425
the right answer
versus when they might

554
00:26:19,425 --> 00:26:22,760
be giving the wrong answer.

555
00:26:22,760 --> 00:26:25,400
And there's research that shows
that if you take a pre-trained

556
00:26:25,400 --> 00:26:29,280
model and plot the confidence
of the model versus the accuracy

557
00:26:29,280 --> 00:26:30,043
of the model--

558
00:26:30,043 --> 00:26:31,960
so the confidence is the
predicted probability

559
00:26:31,960 --> 00:26:33,680
of the answer that
it gives- and this

560
00:26:33,680 --> 00:26:37,700
is the actual accuracy of
those answers on average,

561
00:26:37,700 --> 00:26:39,700
you see that it's actually
very well calibrated.

562
00:26:39,700 --> 00:26:42,160
So that confidence actually
reflects its accuracy

563
00:26:42,160 --> 00:26:43,880
really well.

564
00:26:43,880 --> 00:26:46,420
But then after post-training
with reinforcement learning,

565
00:26:46,420 --> 00:26:52,400
we actually see that the model
becomes far less calibrated.

566
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:55,600
And so maybe
reinforcement learning,

567
00:26:55,600 --> 00:26:57,060
while it's making
the model better,

568
00:26:57,060 --> 00:27:00,183
is actually deteriorating
some of the calibration

569
00:27:00,183 --> 00:27:02,100
that these models might
have at the beginning.

570
00:27:02,100 --> 00:27:05,080

571
00:27:05,080 --> 00:27:07,200
Perhaps one direction,
there was some research

572
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:11,030
by Tian et al. that showed that
if you verbalize the probability

573
00:27:11,030 --> 00:27:14,010
rather than using the
probability like the logits

574
00:27:14,010 --> 00:27:17,630
in the model and encourage it
to provide multiple guesses,

575
00:27:17,630 --> 00:27:22,210
then you can get more calibrated
model performance after RLHF

576
00:27:22,210 --> 00:27:24,530
compared to the RLHF model.

577
00:27:24,530 --> 00:27:26,430
So maybe that's one
possible direction.

578
00:27:26,430 --> 00:27:27,805
Even then, you
need to figure out

579
00:27:27,805 --> 00:27:29,390
how this would
interface with a human

580
00:27:29,390 --> 00:27:32,930
and then evaluate how it does
when the human and model are

581
00:27:32,930 --> 00:27:35,362
working together.

582
00:27:35,362 --> 00:27:37,570
But yeah, this is, I think,
an important open problem

583
00:27:37,570 --> 00:27:40,970
for thinking about
how humans and AI

584
00:27:40,970 --> 00:27:44,210
systems are working together.

585
00:27:44,210 --> 00:27:47,090
And then even separate from
that, a lot of AI systems

586
00:27:47,090 --> 00:27:50,382
are not going to have a human
there at the end of the day that

587
00:27:50,382 --> 00:27:51,590
are checking all the answers.

588
00:27:51,590 --> 00:27:53,512
And this is true in
autonomous driving,

589
00:27:53,512 --> 00:27:55,970
where a human isn't going to
be always holding the steering

590
00:27:55,970 --> 00:27:56,470
wheel.

591
00:27:56,470 --> 00:27:58,730
This is true in
robotics, where maybe you

592
00:27:58,730 --> 00:28:00,310
will be working with a human.

593
00:28:00,310 --> 00:28:02,010
But probably, many
of the decisions

594
00:28:02,010 --> 00:28:05,010
won't be checked by a human
compared to many of the language

595
00:28:05,010 --> 00:28:06,060
model use cases.

596
00:28:06,060 --> 00:28:08,310
And even in language models,
there's lots of use cases

597
00:28:08,310 --> 00:28:13,656
where you want to just plug
that in to another system

598
00:28:13,656 --> 00:28:15,010
to control some system.

599
00:28:15,010 --> 00:28:18,590
Maybe web agents, for example,
will be autonomously choosing

600
00:28:18,590 --> 00:28:20,390
what to do.

601
00:28:20,390 --> 00:28:24,190
And so in those scenarios, can
we get really high reliability?

602
00:28:24,190 --> 00:28:26,970
Reinforcement learning is
likely a part of the solution.

603
00:28:26,970 --> 00:28:30,750
Online data is really helpful
for getting high performance.

604
00:28:30,750 --> 00:28:33,630
And there's promising results
that reinforcement learning can

605
00:28:33,630 --> 00:28:36,070
give really high reliability.

606
00:28:36,070 --> 00:28:38,070
One example by Luo et al.

607
00:28:38,070 --> 00:28:41,050
showed that on fairly
challenging robot tasks,

608
00:28:41,050 --> 00:28:46,150
they're able to get performance
where they ran the policy 100

609
00:28:46,150 --> 00:28:48,830
times and it worked 100 times.

610
00:28:48,830 --> 00:28:50,310
So that's pretty cool.

611
00:28:50,310 --> 00:28:52,550
But extending this to
large-scale scenarios

612
00:28:52,550 --> 00:28:54,950
where you need to have
really high reliability

613
00:28:54,950 --> 00:28:56,090
is quite important.

614
00:28:56,090 --> 00:29:00,002

615
00:29:00,002 --> 00:29:06,760
And then the last thing I'll
talk about is evaluation.

616
00:29:06,760 --> 00:29:11,460
So in supervised learning,
we can measure accuracy.

617
00:29:11,460 --> 00:29:13,260
And we can use held
out validation sets.

618
00:29:13,260 --> 00:29:15,010
And this is kind of
like heaven because we

619
00:29:15,010 --> 00:29:17,760
can get a very nice
measure offline

620
00:29:17,760 --> 00:29:20,560
of how good our model is.

621
00:29:20,560 --> 00:29:22,460
But in reinforcement
learning in general,

622
00:29:22,460 --> 00:29:25,220
there aren't any
reliable offline metrics.

623
00:29:25,220 --> 00:29:27,020
And to evaluate
performance, typically,

624
00:29:27,020 --> 00:29:29,640
at the end of the day, you
just need to run your policy,

625
00:29:29,640 --> 00:29:32,120
roll it out in whatever
environment you're in,

626
00:29:32,120 --> 00:29:37,080
and measure the reward or
measure the performance.

627
00:29:37,080 --> 00:29:43,000
And the reason for this is that
this data collected so far,

628
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:46,640
under whatever policy, differs
from the data that was collected

629
00:29:46,640 --> 00:29:48,480
by the learned policy.

630
00:29:48,480 --> 00:29:52,920
And so it's really hard to
evaluate the policy on states

631
00:29:52,920 --> 00:29:54,680
that the policy
is going to visit

632
00:29:54,680 --> 00:29:56,520
when you don't have
those set of states

633
00:29:56,520 --> 00:29:58,810
that the policy will
necessarily visit.

634
00:29:58,810 --> 00:30:01,320

635
00:30:01,320 --> 00:30:05,170
Now, to make matters worse, if
we have a generalized system

636
00:30:05,170 --> 00:30:09,530
that is intended not just to do
one task but to do many tasks,

637
00:30:09,530 --> 00:30:11,490
then we need to
evaluate our policy

638
00:30:11,490 --> 00:30:15,027
not just in one circumstance but
in all sorts of circumstances.

639
00:30:15,027 --> 00:30:16,610
And this means that
we need to collect

640
00:30:16,610 --> 00:30:21,210
large amounts of online
data and evaluate all

641
00:30:21,210 --> 00:30:22,970
of those different rollouts.

642
00:30:22,970 --> 00:30:24,890
And this makes it really
hard to decide first

643
00:30:24,890 --> 00:30:28,370
if the policy is good enough
to deploy or even just

644
00:30:28,370 --> 00:30:32,650
the simpler problem of picking
which of your different models

645
00:30:32,650 --> 00:30:38,250
is the best model and choosing
hyperparameters, for example.

646
00:30:38,250 --> 00:30:41,890
As a real-world circumstance
of this problem,

647
00:30:41,890 --> 00:30:45,450
about a month ago,
OpenAI released

648
00:30:45,450 --> 00:30:51,730
a ChatGPT model, a new
version of GPT-4o, I think.

649
00:30:51,730 --> 00:30:55,730
And if you asked
it, how many people

650
00:30:55,730 --> 00:30:58,370
am I better than at thinking?

651
00:30:58,370 --> 00:31:00,350
it said, if I had to
put a number on it,

652
00:31:00,350 --> 00:31:03,350
I'd estimate that you're
easily in the 130 to 145

653
00:31:03,350 --> 00:31:08,790
range of IQ, which would put
you above about 98% to 99.7%

654
00:31:08,790 --> 00:31:10,320
of people in raw
thinking ability.

655
00:31:10,320 --> 00:31:12,070
But honestly, comparing
you to most people

656
00:31:12,070 --> 00:31:16,070
almost insults the quality of
mind you're aiming to develop.

657
00:31:16,070 --> 00:31:19,470
So maybe this isn't what you're
kind of expecting or looking

658
00:31:19,470 --> 00:31:21,850
for in your model.

659
00:31:21,850 --> 00:31:23,790
And OpenAI actually
ended up needing

660
00:31:23,790 --> 00:31:27,950
to rollback this update that
made the bot excessively

661
00:31:27,950 --> 00:31:30,893
flattering to users.

662
00:31:30,893 --> 00:31:32,310
This gets at the
challenge of it's

663
00:31:32,310 --> 00:31:36,195
really hard to know if the model
that you're developing is good

664
00:31:36,195 --> 00:31:38,570
enough, is better than your
previous model, and so forth.

665
00:31:38,570 --> 00:31:40,440
It's really hard to
evaluate these systems.

666
00:31:40,440 --> 00:31:43,750

667
00:31:43,750 --> 00:31:46,110
A couple specific
open questions.

668
00:31:46,110 --> 00:31:49,190
First, can we develop
offline metrics that

669
00:31:49,190 --> 00:31:51,670
can at least rule
out bad models,

670
00:31:51,670 --> 00:31:53,630
let alone estimate performance?

671
00:31:53,630 --> 00:31:57,382
Estimating performance
would be the dream.

672
00:31:57,382 --> 00:31:59,590
But at the very least, maybe
we could rule out models

673
00:31:59,590 --> 00:32:03,800
that we shouldn't
deploy offline.

674
00:32:03,800 --> 00:32:06,500
And then even in terms of
online policy evaluation,

675
00:32:06,500 --> 00:32:09,620
if we need to evaluate a policy
in all sorts of circumstances,

676
00:32:09,620 --> 00:32:13,800
can we figure out how best to
select representative scenarios

677
00:32:13,800 --> 00:32:16,280
that will allow us
to measure generalist

678
00:32:16,280 --> 00:32:18,800
performance without
having to evaluate

679
00:32:18,800 --> 00:32:20,430
every single possible scenario?

680
00:32:20,430 --> 00:32:23,480

681
00:32:23,480 --> 00:32:27,700
Now, that was all I had for
frontiers and open problems.

682
00:32:27,700 --> 00:32:30,880
And I guess one thing
I want to convey

683
00:32:30,880 --> 00:32:33,048
is that now that
you've taken the class,

684
00:32:33,048 --> 00:32:34,840
you should be really
well equipped to start

685
00:32:34,840 --> 00:32:36,248
tackling these challenges.

686
00:32:36,248 --> 00:32:38,540
You don't know everything
about reinforcement learning,

687
00:32:38,540 --> 00:32:39,060
of course.

688
00:32:39,060 --> 00:32:43,120
But you should know a lot
of the fundamentals that

689
00:32:43,120 --> 00:32:45,220
will allow you to go
read research papers,

690
00:32:45,220 --> 00:32:48,800
understand where the
state of the art is,

691
00:32:48,800 --> 00:32:51,520
and build on the
state of the art.

692
00:32:51,520 --> 00:32:54,760
So your question is, why
does this happen basically?

693
00:32:54,760 --> 00:32:56,660
So I don't think we
necessarily expect this.

694
00:32:56,660 --> 00:32:59,400
But looking at
this in retrospect,

695
00:32:59,400 --> 00:33:02,900
I think it's reasonable to
make some guesses for why

696
00:33:02,900 --> 00:33:03,680
this is happening.

697
00:33:03,680 --> 00:33:06,340
And I think the predominant
theory for why this is happening

698
00:33:06,340 --> 00:33:10,720
is that the objective that
you're optimizing for, first,

699
00:33:10,720 --> 00:33:12,220
is not exactly accuracy.

700
00:33:12,220 --> 00:33:14,320
It's not exactly
aligned with that.

701
00:33:14,320 --> 00:33:17,660
And typically,
what reinforcement

702
00:33:17,660 --> 00:33:20,060
learning with human feedback--
what it's doing oftentimes

703
00:33:20,060 --> 00:33:22,300
is actually sharpening
the distribution.

704
00:33:22,300 --> 00:33:24,100
So the pre-trained
model, it often

705
00:33:24,100 --> 00:33:25,900
will output all sorts of stuff.

706
00:33:25,900 --> 00:33:28,537
And what RL is trying
to do is-- let's

707
00:33:28,537 --> 00:33:30,620
say this is the mode that
we really want our model

708
00:33:30,620 --> 00:33:31,600
to be predicting.

709
00:33:31,600 --> 00:33:33,392
And it's to be sharpening
that distribution

710
00:33:33,392 --> 00:33:36,500
to really encourage the model
to be more likely to sample one

711
00:33:36,500 --> 00:33:37,660
mode.

712
00:33:37,660 --> 00:33:40,780
And that sharpening
often leads to it

713
00:33:40,780 --> 00:33:44,860
being overconfident in certain
circumstances, especially

714
00:33:44,860 --> 00:33:52,220
when preferences are rewarding
you for being confident

715
00:33:52,220 --> 00:33:55,700
and then also, at times
being correct and confident

716
00:33:55,700 --> 00:33:58,010
and sometimes being
incorrect and confident.

717
00:33:58,010 --> 00:34:00,770

718
00:34:00,770 --> 00:34:04,670
So let's talk a little bit
about how to do research.

719
00:34:04,670 --> 00:34:09,110
And this will be a focus on
how to do research that's

720
00:34:09,110 --> 00:34:12,350
more of an empirical flavor
similar to the research

721
00:34:12,350 --> 00:34:14,830
that you do if you were doing
a deep reinforcement learning

722
00:34:14,830 --> 00:34:17,110
project.

723
00:34:17,110 --> 00:34:20,110
Now, before we get
started, I have

724
00:34:20,110 --> 00:34:22,790
a couple different thoughts.

725
00:34:22,790 --> 00:34:25,510
The first is that
I'll talk a lot

726
00:34:25,510 --> 00:34:28,230
about how I go about the
research process and advice

727
00:34:28,230 --> 00:34:30,630
that I give to PhD
students as I see

728
00:34:30,630 --> 00:34:33,590
them working through
various research projects.

729
00:34:33,590 --> 00:34:35,870
But there's many different
ways to do research.

730
00:34:35,870 --> 00:34:38,550
And it's important and
good for the community

731
00:34:38,550 --> 00:34:40,570
to do research in
many different ways

732
00:34:40,570 --> 00:34:43,150
because that leads to
more diverse discoveries.

733
00:34:43,150 --> 00:34:46,250
And if everyone did research the
exact way that I do research,

734
00:34:46,250 --> 00:34:50,770
then we would probably have
fewer diverse discoveries.

735
00:34:50,770 --> 00:34:52,449
And so diversity is good.

736
00:34:52,449 --> 00:34:57,932
And my research approach is not
necessarily the best approach.

737
00:34:57,932 --> 00:34:59,890
And I don't think there's
any one approach that

738
00:34:59,890 --> 00:35:01,742
is the best approach.

739
00:35:01,742 --> 00:35:03,450
I also felt like it
could be good to give

740
00:35:03,450 --> 00:35:06,410
a little bit of a backstory,
which is that I did not intend

741
00:35:06,410 --> 00:35:08,330
to have a career in research.

742
00:35:08,330 --> 00:35:14,090
I was planning to go to
industry and develop products,

743
00:35:14,090 --> 00:35:17,972
which is what my parents did
in various engineering fields.

744
00:35:17,972 --> 00:35:20,430
And I felt like that was the
way to have real-world impact.

745
00:35:20,430 --> 00:35:25,850
I did internships in industry
and eventually figured out

746
00:35:25,850 --> 00:35:28,410
that I was excited about AI.

747
00:35:28,410 --> 00:35:30,490
And I was excited about
algorithm development.

748
00:35:30,490 --> 00:35:33,170
And it seemed like the
frontier of that and algorithm

749
00:35:33,170 --> 00:35:35,730
development was really
in a research environment

750
00:35:35,730 --> 00:35:38,050
rather than in an
industry environment.

751
00:35:38,050 --> 00:35:40,810
And a lot of the topics
I was excited about

752
00:35:40,810 --> 00:35:41,970
didn't really work.

753
00:35:41,970 --> 00:35:44,190
Things like robotics
didn't really work.

754
00:35:44,190 --> 00:35:47,330
Even today, robots often
don't work very well.

755
00:35:47,330 --> 00:35:50,410
And so they're really in
this sort of research domain.

756
00:35:50,410 --> 00:35:52,610
Also, the people that
I met in industry

757
00:35:52,610 --> 00:35:54,380
that were working on
the kinds of things

758
00:35:54,380 --> 00:35:56,467
that I wanted to be
working on all had PhDs.

759
00:35:56,467 --> 00:35:58,800
And so I was like, well, I
should probably get a PhD too

760
00:35:58,800 --> 00:36:01,100
if I want to work
on those things.

761
00:36:01,100 --> 00:36:03,420
And then I also just found
research and the ambiguity

762
00:36:03,420 --> 00:36:06,260
of research to be really
kind of intellectually

763
00:36:06,260 --> 00:36:08,940
interesting and challenging.

764
00:36:08,940 --> 00:36:10,740
And so that's all
to say that you

765
00:36:10,740 --> 00:36:14,140
don't have to intend to
have to work in research

766
00:36:14,140 --> 00:36:16,590
in order to eventually
end up doing research.

767
00:36:16,590 --> 00:36:19,760

768
00:36:19,760 --> 00:36:21,260
And then the last
thing I'll mention

769
00:36:21,260 --> 00:36:25,220
before getting into
how to do research

770
00:36:25,220 --> 00:36:30,620
is just like some general
realities with research.

771
00:36:30,620 --> 00:36:35,500
The first is that very, very
few research ideas are good.

772
00:36:35,500 --> 00:36:37,620
And very, very
few research ideas

773
00:36:37,620 --> 00:36:41,060
have impact, have lasting
impact especially.

774
00:36:41,060 --> 00:36:42,820
A lot of ideas don't work.

775
00:36:42,820 --> 00:36:45,300
A lot of ideas don't
work to the extent

776
00:36:45,300 --> 00:36:46,940
that they won't lead to papers.

777
00:36:46,940 --> 00:36:50,060
As an example of this,
here are the outcomes

778
00:36:50,060 --> 00:36:52,080
of some of my research ideas.

779
00:36:52,080 --> 00:36:54,640
And here are the outcome of
some more of my research ideas.

780
00:36:54,640 --> 00:36:58,040
And there's a lot of failure.

781
00:36:58,040 --> 00:37:03,080
And the risk and the
challenge of doing research

782
00:37:03,080 --> 00:37:04,925
informs different ways--

783
00:37:04,925 --> 00:37:06,800
a lot of what I'll say
has to do with dealing

784
00:37:06,800 --> 00:37:10,960
with that sort of risk and
dealing with those challenges.

785
00:37:10,960 --> 00:37:14,920
And then even if it does lead
to a paper, a lot of papers

786
00:37:14,920 --> 00:37:18,823
won't necessarily
have a lasting impact.

787
00:37:18,823 --> 00:37:20,240
The second thing
that I'll mention

788
00:37:20,240 --> 00:37:24,740
is that research is
inherently incremental.

789
00:37:24,740 --> 00:37:27,720
We are building upon the
knowledge of previous research

790
00:37:27,720 --> 00:37:29,080
that's been done.

791
00:37:29,080 --> 00:37:31,920
And as an example
of this, AlphaFold,

792
00:37:31,920 --> 00:37:37,200
arguably like a really impactful
thing, very, very successful,

793
00:37:37,200 --> 00:37:40,720
arguably could be
considered a breakthrough.

794
00:37:40,720 --> 00:37:43,460
This itself was, in
many ways, incremental.

795
00:37:43,460 --> 00:37:47,480
It built very closely on a large
20-year academic project called

796
00:37:47,480 --> 00:37:52,370
CASP, which was a protein
structure prediction project

797
00:37:52,370 --> 00:37:55,450
that was basically kind of
a competition and a data set

798
00:37:55,450 --> 00:37:58,490
for doing this sort of structure
prediction and also built very

799
00:37:58,490 --> 00:38:00,470
heavily on advances
in neural networks.

800
00:38:00,470 --> 00:38:04,090
And so even if any one research
project isn't a breakthrough,

801
00:38:04,090 --> 00:38:07,210
it often plays an important role
because it allows other projects

802
00:38:07,210 --> 00:38:10,170
to build on it or other projects
to potentially rule out ideas

803
00:38:10,170 --> 00:38:12,142
that have been tried already.

804
00:38:12,142 --> 00:38:13,850
And then the last
thing that I'll mention

805
00:38:13,850 --> 00:38:17,410
is that oftentimes people
think about developing

806
00:38:17,410 --> 00:38:19,350
really cool, fancy algorithms.

807
00:38:19,350 --> 00:38:24,190
But in a world where scaling
up is often quite important,

808
00:38:24,190 --> 00:38:27,450
especially in AI
research, oftentimes

809
00:38:27,450 --> 00:38:29,010
it's actually the
simple ideas that

810
00:38:29,010 --> 00:38:33,910
have more impact than the fancy
ideas or the complex algorithms.

811
00:38:33,910 --> 00:38:36,632
And that's just because the
simple ideas are the things that

812
00:38:36,632 --> 00:38:38,090
can scale, that
are the things that

813
00:38:38,090 --> 00:38:41,570
are easy to be integrated
into different systems,

814
00:38:41,570 --> 00:38:43,870
and easy to build on top of.

815
00:38:43,870 --> 00:38:48,450

816
00:38:48,450 --> 00:38:50,350
With that out of
the way, I'll talk

817
00:38:50,350 --> 00:38:53,550
about what to work on,
how to do the work,

818
00:38:53,550 --> 00:38:58,430
how to share the work, and then
a couple miscellaneous topics.

819
00:38:58,430 --> 00:39:02,750
And I would argue that
these three things--

820
00:39:02,750 --> 00:39:05,110
picking problems to work
on, doing the research,

821
00:39:05,110 --> 00:39:06,550
and sharing the work--

822
00:39:06,550 --> 00:39:08,590
are all equally important.

823
00:39:08,590 --> 00:39:11,670
And it's easy to think that
maybe number 2 is the most

824
00:39:11,670 --> 00:39:13,020
important or maybe number 1.

825
00:39:13,020 --> 00:39:15,270
But all of these are actually
really, really important

826
00:39:15,270 --> 00:39:17,040
for being successful
in research.

827
00:39:17,040 --> 00:39:20,136

828
00:39:20,136 --> 00:39:23,670
So step 1 for what to work on--

829
00:39:23,670 --> 00:39:26,550
I think that a research
project needs two things.

830
00:39:26,550 --> 00:39:29,310
The first is an
important problem.

831
00:39:29,310 --> 00:39:33,310
And the second is a plan for
how to approach that problem.

832
00:39:33,310 --> 00:39:36,190
And if you only have
one of these, then

833
00:39:36,190 --> 00:39:39,390
usually that doesn't constitute
a good project direction.

834
00:39:39,390 --> 00:39:41,950
So for example, maybe
one project idea

835
00:39:41,950 --> 00:39:44,790
is I want to solve
climate change.

836
00:39:44,790 --> 00:39:47,000
That is an important problem.

837
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:49,440
But I don't have a plan
for how to approach it.

838
00:39:49,440 --> 00:39:51,340
So it doesn't make a
good research project

839
00:39:51,340 --> 00:39:54,880
even though it's a
really important problem.

840
00:39:54,880 --> 00:39:57,640
Second, maybe I have a
really cool algorithm

841
00:39:57,640 --> 00:40:00,800
that at best will make a robot
or a reinforcement learning

842
00:40:00,800 --> 00:40:03,800
system a little bit
more successful.

843
00:40:03,800 --> 00:40:07,420
This is maybe a
really cool idea.

844
00:40:07,420 --> 00:40:08,420
And maybe I have a plan.

845
00:40:08,420 --> 00:40:09,280
Maybe I have two.

846
00:40:09,280 --> 00:40:11,420
But I'm missing the
important problem

847
00:40:11,420 --> 00:40:12,670
that this is actually solving.

848
00:40:12,670 --> 00:40:15,880

849
00:40:15,880 --> 00:40:17,498
And so for this
latter part, I think

850
00:40:17,498 --> 00:40:19,040
it's really important
to think about,

851
00:40:19,040 --> 00:40:22,127
if the project is really,
really successful,

852
00:40:22,127 --> 00:40:23,460
what will the outcome look like?

853
00:40:23,460 --> 00:40:24,918
Does it solve an
important problem?

854
00:40:24,918 --> 00:40:27,360
Or what is the upper
bound of that project?

855
00:40:27,360 --> 00:40:30,520
And if the upper bound
is not very high, then

856
00:40:30,520 --> 00:40:32,710
usually it doesn't make
for a very good project.

857
00:40:32,710 --> 00:40:35,360

858
00:40:35,360 --> 00:40:37,640
Step 2, if you have an
important problem and a plan

859
00:40:37,640 --> 00:40:42,440
for how to approach it,
are you excited about it?

860
00:40:42,440 --> 00:40:44,540
Research is a lot of work.

861
00:40:44,540 --> 00:40:48,720
And if it's fun, if you think
it's really cool and exciting,

862
00:40:48,720 --> 00:40:50,880
then you're more likely
to work hard on it.

863
00:40:50,880 --> 00:40:53,120
And if you're
working hard on it,

864
00:40:53,120 --> 00:40:56,410
you're much more likely
to be successful on it.

865
00:40:56,410 --> 00:40:58,660
And this is actually probably
true for a lot of things

866
00:40:58,660 --> 00:41:00,140
outside of research too.

867
00:41:00,140 --> 00:41:03,660
It's good to spend
time on the things

868
00:41:03,660 --> 00:41:06,380
that you find exciting,
that you find really cool.

869
00:41:06,380 --> 00:41:08,780
Probably, if you can find
some of those things--

870
00:41:08,780 --> 00:41:10,385
they may not even
be just one thing.

871
00:41:10,385 --> 00:41:12,260
If you can just find
something that you think

872
00:41:12,260 --> 00:41:14,020
is really cool
and exciting, then

873
00:41:14,020 --> 00:41:18,100
you'll probably do
a lot better at it.

874
00:41:18,100 --> 00:41:23,380
And then step 3, like I
mentioned before, a lot of ideas

875
00:41:23,380 --> 00:41:24,460
don't work.

876
00:41:24,460 --> 00:41:26,180
And so it's really
good to think about,

877
00:41:26,180 --> 00:41:30,340
if you are brutally honest
about why your idea could fail

878
00:41:30,340 --> 00:41:33,100
to solve the problem,
does the idea still

879
00:41:33,100 --> 00:41:35,540
have a good chance of working?

880
00:41:35,540 --> 00:41:39,580
And if not, then
probably it won't work.

881
00:41:39,580 --> 00:41:43,195
And that's mostly just because
of the reality of most ideas

882
00:41:43,195 --> 00:41:44,570
not actually
working in research.

883
00:41:44,570 --> 00:41:46,445
And it's important to
be honest with yourself

884
00:41:46,445 --> 00:41:49,170
about whether this will actually
solve the problem or not.

885
00:41:49,170 --> 00:41:51,790

886
00:41:51,790 --> 00:41:55,310
So that's my three-step
process for picking problems.

887
00:41:55,310 --> 00:41:57,390
Now, a couple more
thoughts on this.

888
00:41:57,390 --> 00:42:04,150
So research can often either
start with an idea or a problem.

889
00:42:04,150 --> 00:42:08,230
And I think both of these sorts
of approaches to a project

890
00:42:08,230 --> 00:42:09,450
have merits.

891
00:42:09,450 --> 00:42:11,730
And I've done research
that's more idea-driven.

892
00:42:11,730 --> 00:42:14,510
I've also done research
that's more h driven.

893
00:42:14,510 --> 00:42:17,830
But I tend to lean
towards one myself.

894
00:42:17,830 --> 00:42:20,730
And the reason is as follows.

895
00:42:20,730 --> 00:42:23,190
So if you start
with an idea, you

896
00:42:23,190 --> 00:42:27,230
need to find a problem
that that idea solves.

897
00:42:27,230 --> 00:42:29,430
And there may not exist
an important problem

898
00:42:29,430 --> 00:42:31,190
that the idea solves.

899
00:42:31,190 --> 00:42:33,530
It may solve a problem.

900
00:42:33,530 --> 00:42:35,990
But there might be a better
way to solve the problem.

901
00:42:35,990 --> 00:42:38,050
And if there's a better
way to solve the problem,

902
00:42:38,050 --> 00:42:42,680
then that idea may not
have a lot of impact.

903
00:42:42,680 --> 00:42:44,900
On the contrary, if you
start with a problem,

904
00:42:44,900 --> 00:42:48,080
then you're trying to find the
best solution to that problem.

905
00:42:48,080 --> 00:42:51,560
And one downside is it may
be harder to write a paper

906
00:42:51,560 --> 00:42:55,560
if the solution ends up being
kind of obvious in retrospect.

907
00:42:55,560 --> 00:42:57,720
But at least you're
guaranteed to be working

908
00:42:57,720 --> 00:42:59,400
on an important problem.

909
00:42:59,400 --> 00:43:01,040
And you find a solution.

910
00:43:01,040 --> 00:43:02,815
You'll still be happy
even if it ends up

911
00:43:02,815 --> 00:43:04,440
being a little bit
harder to figure out

912
00:43:04,440 --> 00:43:07,480
how to write a paper about it.

913
00:43:07,480 --> 00:43:10,440
Another way to put this is
that with idea-driven research,

914
00:43:10,440 --> 00:43:12,880
your goal is to get
the idea to work.

915
00:43:12,880 --> 00:43:15,880
Whereas in the second case, your
goal is to solve the problem.

916
00:43:15,880 --> 00:43:19,560
And the second camp seems like
a much more comfortable place

917
00:43:19,560 --> 00:43:23,045
to be because it's
possible that the idea just

918
00:43:23,045 --> 00:43:23,920
doesn't want to work.

919
00:43:23,920 --> 00:43:28,120
Or it's not going to work
at the end of the day.

920
00:43:28,120 --> 00:43:31,640
Another note here is that if
you're thinking about a problem

921
00:43:31,640 --> 00:43:34,560
that you want to solve,
it's really important

922
00:43:34,560 --> 00:43:37,240
to get to the bottleneck
of that problem.

923
00:43:37,240 --> 00:43:40,000
So if the problem is
bottlenecked on data,

924
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:41,980
for example, and
you start exploring

925
00:43:41,980 --> 00:43:45,500
new algorithms separate from
the data for how to solve it,

926
00:43:45,500 --> 00:43:48,740
then you actually probably
won't see performance gains

927
00:43:48,740 --> 00:43:53,100
from working on methods if
it's bottlenecked by data.

928
00:43:53,100 --> 00:43:55,140
And most, maybe you'll
see a couple percentage

929
00:43:55,140 --> 00:43:57,620
points because, really, the
big performance gains are

930
00:43:57,620 --> 00:43:59,460
going to be on the data side.

931
00:43:59,460 --> 00:44:00,900
And so if you pick
the things that

932
00:44:00,900 --> 00:44:03,460
are bottlenecks and the things
that are actually preventing

933
00:44:03,460 --> 00:44:05,980
that thing from
working, then you're

934
00:44:05,980 --> 00:44:08,280
more likely to be
able to make progress.

935
00:44:08,280 --> 00:44:11,340
And so I think a lot of
problem-driven research

936
00:44:11,340 --> 00:44:13,900
ends up thinking about what
is the bottleneck for solving

937
00:44:13,900 --> 00:44:15,500
this problem.

938
00:44:15,500 --> 00:44:17,900
And this can also be even
in terms of much longer term

939
00:44:17,900 --> 00:44:18,400
thinking.

940
00:44:18,400 --> 00:44:25,140
You can think about, I want to
develop an LLM that can solve--

941
00:44:25,140 --> 00:44:27,780

942
00:44:27,780 --> 00:44:31,120
it can prove something that
humans have never proved before,

943
00:44:31,120 --> 00:44:33,020
like P versus NP or something.

944
00:44:33,020 --> 00:44:36,700
Or I want to get a robot that
can make a bowl of cereal

945
00:44:36,700 --> 00:44:38,790
in a new kitchen.

946
00:44:38,790 --> 00:44:43,670
Those are things that you can't
solve in any individual research

947
00:44:43,670 --> 00:44:44,572
project.

948
00:44:44,572 --> 00:44:46,030
But you can think
about, OK, what's

949
00:44:46,030 --> 00:44:47,405
the bottleneck
for getting there?

950
00:44:47,405 --> 00:44:49,590
And can I work on the
part of that problem

951
00:44:49,590 --> 00:44:52,150
in my next project
that will make progress

952
00:44:52,150 --> 00:44:54,390
on that larger goal?

953
00:44:54,390 --> 00:44:56,750
A couple more things.

954
00:44:56,750 --> 00:44:59,590
For this next thing, I want to
tell a little bit of a story.

955
00:44:59,590 --> 00:45:03,110
So in the second year of my
PhD, I did an internship.

956
00:45:03,110 --> 00:45:04,630
And I did an
internship at Google

957
00:45:04,630 --> 00:45:06,570
where they had all these robots.

958
00:45:06,570 --> 00:45:09,870
And I wanted to use the
robots to do something cool.

959
00:45:09,870 --> 00:45:12,910
And my project idea was to
build a predictive model,

960
00:45:12,910 --> 00:45:15,010
actually a video
generation model,

961
00:45:15,010 --> 00:45:17,600
and use it to learn
skills across the robots.

962
00:45:17,600 --> 00:45:20,310

963
00:45:20,310 --> 00:45:21,950
And then there was a problem.

964
00:45:21,950 --> 00:45:25,150
Most research projects have
problems that you find.

965
00:45:25,150 --> 00:45:28,590
And in particular, I discovered
that video generation models

966
00:45:28,590 --> 00:45:30,590
were really bad.

967
00:45:30,590 --> 00:45:34,430
So here was a video that
I collected on the robot.

968
00:45:34,430 --> 00:45:39,650
And here was a prediction from
arguably the state of the art

969
00:45:39,650 --> 00:45:42,010
video generation model.

970
00:45:42,010 --> 00:45:44,570
And you can see
that it is erasing

971
00:45:44,570 --> 00:45:47,770
all the objects in the video
and is barely capturing

972
00:45:47,770 --> 00:45:49,170
the motion of the arm.

973
00:45:49,170 --> 00:45:52,770
Doesn't even capture the
background correctly.

974
00:45:52,770 --> 00:45:55,610
And so this means that I
wasn't able to actually use

975
00:45:55,610 --> 00:45:57,930
these kinds of models to
actually get the robot

976
00:45:57,930 --> 00:46:01,130
to do something better.

977
00:46:01,130 --> 00:46:03,810
Now, at the time, I
kind of viewed myself

978
00:46:03,810 --> 00:46:06,570
as a machine learning and
robotics person and not really

979
00:46:06,570 --> 00:46:08,250
a computer vision
person, not a person

980
00:46:08,250 --> 00:46:10,530
who works on generative models.

981
00:46:10,530 --> 00:46:13,438
If I kind of felt like I should
only work on machine learning

982
00:46:13,438 --> 00:46:15,730
and robotics, I might have
to table this and figure out

983
00:46:15,730 --> 00:46:17,490
a different project to work on.

984
00:46:17,490 --> 00:46:22,330
But instead, I decided to work
on trying to develop a better

985
00:46:22,330 --> 00:46:24,210
video generation model.

986
00:46:24,210 --> 00:46:26,145
Now, arguably, I
wasn't necessarily

987
00:46:26,145 --> 00:46:27,270
the best person to do this.

988
00:46:27,270 --> 00:46:29,230
I had never built a
generative model before.

989
00:46:29,230 --> 00:46:30,730
I didn't really
know that much about

990
00:46:30,730 --> 00:46:32,210
architectures for doing so.

991
00:46:32,210 --> 00:46:34,235
But not a lot of people
were working on it

992
00:46:34,235 --> 00:46:35,860
at the time because
they didn't realize

993
00:46:35,860 --> 00:46:37,620
how bad these models were.

994
00:46:37,620 --> 00:46:40,580
And then the resulting paper
ended up being the basis

995
00:46:40,580 --> 00:46:44,500
for work in my job talk, now
has around 1,300 citations.

996
00:46:44,500 --> 00:46:47,100
And it arguably
informed the community

997
00:46:47,100 --> 00:46:49,220
that it's kind of an
interesting problem to study

998
00:46:49,220 --> 00:46:53,340
and led to much better video
generation models than the one

999
00:46:53,340 --> 00:46:55,780
that I was able to develop.

1000
00:46:55,780 --> 00:46:58,445
And so I guess
the kind of lesson

1001
00:46:58,445 --> 00:46:59,820
that I'm trying
to convey here is

1002
00:46:59,820 --> 00:47:03,440
that it's good to not box
yourself into an area and say,

1003
00:47:03,440 --> 00:47:06,300
I'm only going to work
on this particular area.

1004
00:47:06,300 --> 00:47:09,940
And the reason for that is if
you cross topic boundaries,

1005
00:47:09,940 --> 00:47:12,032
it's going to
inform new problems.

1006
00:47:12,032 --> 00:47:13,740
Because if you're
trying to use something

1007
00:47:13,740 --> 00:47:18,357
that is a problem for advancing
the topic you want to work on,

1008
00:47:18,357 --> 00:47:19,940
then that kind of
informs that there's

1009
00:47:19,940 --> 00:47:21,200
a problem with that
existing thing.

1010
00:47:21,200 --> 00:47:22,700
And oftentimes,
people in that field

1011
00:47:22,700 --> 00:47:24,900
don't know that it's a problem.

1012
00:47:24,900 --> 00:47:26,540
And then it also
brings new ideas

1013
00:47:26,540 --> 00:47:30,430
to that other topic
or domain as well.

1014
00:47:30,430 --> 00:47:32,680
And so I see a lot of people
kind of boxing themselves

1015
00:47:32,680 --> 00:47:33,260
into areas.

1016
00:47:33,260 --> 00:47:36,520
But I think that actually
crossing topic boundaries

1017
00:47:36,520 --> 00:47:41,572
ends up leading to a lot of
new insights and innovations.

1018
00:47:41,572 --> 00:47:43,280
And then the last
thing that I'll mention

1019
00:47:43,280 --> 00:47:45,940
is don't be a perfectionist.

1020
00:47:45,940 --> 00:47:48,760
So I've seen people feel
like they want to pick

1021
00:47:48,760 --> 00:47:50,760
the perfect project to work on.

1022
00:47:50,760 --> 00:47:53,120
But you never know what
the impact of a project

1023
00:47:53,120 --> 00:47:54,560
will be at the outset.

1024
00:47:54,560 --> 00:47:56,680
Otherwise, it
wouldn't be research.

1025
00:47:56,680 --> 00:47:59,360
And so at some point,
you need to just start

1026
00:47:59,360 --> 00:48:04,400
trying different ideas, start
getting going on a project

1027
00:48:04,400 --> 00:48:06,450
without trying to find
the perfect project.

1028
00:48:06,450 --> 00:48:09,680

1029
00:48:09,680 --> 00:48:13,480
So once you have a project
idea or a project direction,

1030
00:48:13,480 --> 00:48:16,200
a key consideration from
there is handling risk.

1031
00:48:16,200 --> 00:48:21,120
So we've talked about how lots
of research ideas don't work.

1032
00:48:21,120 --> 00:48:24,080
How do we deal with that?

1033
00:48:24,080 --> 00:48:27,060
So there's a lot of tricks
for dealing with the risk.

1034
00:48:27,060 --> 00:48:29,875
And not all of them are going to
apply to every single project.

1035
00:48:29,875 --> 00:48:31,250
But these are
tricks that I think

1036
00:48:31,250 --> 00:48:36,850
you should be able to draw upon
to handle the risk in research.

1037
00:48:36,850 --> 00:48:40,010
So the first is,
wherever possible,

1038
00:48:40,010 --> 00:48:42,710
it's good to front load
the risk in the project.

1039
00:48:42,710 --> 00:48:46,530
Think about, what is the reason
why this might not succeed?

1040
00:48:46,530 --> 00:48:48,630
And before developing
infrastructure,

1041
00:48:48,630 --> 00:48:53,290
before coding up the entire
algorithm and so forth,

1042
00:48:53,290 --> 00:48:56,890
if you can formulate and
run simple experiments that

1043
00:48:56,890 --> 00:48:59,410
test the core unknowns
that you have,

1044
00:48:59,410 --> 00:49:03,890
then you can bring the risk to
the beginning of the project.

1045
00:49:03,890 --> 00:49:05,970
And this can be
really uncomfortable.

1046
00:49:05,970 --> 00:49:09,065
Because oftentimes, building
the infrastructure, coding

1047
00:49:09,065 --> 00:49:11,190
the algorithm, whatever,
that's like the easy part.

1048
00:49:11,190 --> 00:49:14,250
That's the part where you can
seemingly make some progress

1049
00:49:14,250 --> 00:49:15,610
on the project.

1050
00:49:15,610 --> 00:49:17,930
But if you do all that
and then, at the end,

1051
00:49:17,930 --> 00:49:21,170
realize that the core
unknown actually ended

1052
00:49:21,170 --> 00:49:24,613
up not working out, then you
wasted all of that time--

1053
00:49:24,613 --> 00:49:26,530
even though it seemingly
seemed like progress,

1054
00:49:26,530 --> 00:49:29,550
you wasted all that
time on that idea

1055
00:49:29,550 --> 00:49:32,750
that wasn't going to pan
out at the end of the day.

1056
00:49:32,750 --> 00:49:35,150
And so instead, if you
can frontload that risk

1057
00:49:35,150 --> 00:49:37,070
and frontload the
unknowns, run experiments

1058
00:49:37,070 --> 00:49:41,350
to test those unknowns, then
you can make sure that--

1059
00:49:41,350 --> 00:49:44,790
if this is applicable,
you can do that first

1060
00:49:44,790 --> 00:49:46,870
and then build out
the infrastructure

1061
00:49:46,870 --> 00:49:48,550
or build out the
algorithm completely

1062
00:49:48,550 --> 00:49:51,360
and run all the experiments
after doing that initial test.

1063
00:49:51,360 --> 00:49:54,190

1064
00:49:54,190 --> 00:49:59,350
Along the same lines,
thinking through how

1065
00:49:59,350 --> 00:50:02,590
to test ideas in the simplest
possible way or the fastest

1066
00:50:02,590 --> 00:50:06,230
possible way takes some skill
and takes some thinking.

1067
00:50:06,230 --> 00:50:09,450
It's often not obvious how to
test a particular hypothesis.

1068
00:50:09,450 --> 00:50:12,370

1069
00:50:12,370 --> 00:50:15,190
So separate from trying to
frontload risk and frontload

1070
00:50:15,190 --> 00:50:20,310
experiments is simply
just iterating a lot.

1071
00:50:20,310 --> 00:50:25,910
So you have to get lucky to
stumble upon a good idea.

1072
00:50:25,910 --> 00:50:27,880
But you can create
luck for yourself

1073
00:50:27,880 --> 00:50:30,200
just by trying
out lots of ideas,

1074
00:50:30,200 --> 00:50:32,680
by iterating on lots
of different ideas.

1075
00:50:32,680 --> 00:50:36,717
And if you try out an idea and
the targeted experiment doesn't

1076
00:50:36,717 --> 00:50:38,300
work, then you can
discard it quickly,

1077
00:50:38,300 --> 00:50:40,800
try out another
idea, and so forth.

1078
00:50:40,800 --> 00:50:43,640
And for me, there was kind
of lots of times in my PhD

1079
00:50:43,640 --> 00:50:48,740
where I tried out lots of ideas
that didn't make it into papers.

1080
00:50:48,740 --> 00:50:50,400
They failed.

1081
00:50:50,400 --> 00:50:56,120
And I think that this allowed
me to come upon some ideas that

1082
00:50:56,120 --> 00:50:56,660
worked well.

1083
00:50:56,660 --> 00:50:57,660
I didn't always do this.

1084
00:50:57,660 --> 00:51:00,960
Sometimes, I ended up banging
my head against the wall

1085
00:51:00,960 --> 00:51:01,740
plenty of times.

1086
00:51:01,740 --> 00:51:03,157
But this is something
that I found

1087
00:51:03,157 --> 00:51:05,720
to be a good strategy for
trying to iterate and get

1088
00:51:05,720 --> 00:51:07,800
through the bad ideas
quickly so that you

1089
00:51:07,800 --> 00:51:09,310
can get to some good ideas.

1090
00:51:09,310 --> 00:51:12,960

1091
00:51:12,960 --> 00:51:15,080
And then I think the
last thing on this slide

1092
00:51:15,080 --> 00:51:18,300
is if you can try out
lots of ideas, especially

1093
00:51:18,300 --> 00:51:19,800
at the beginning
of the project, you

1094
00:51:19,800 --> 00:51:21,640
can try out lots of
ideas at the beginning

1095
00:51:21,640 --> 00:51:24,620
and then not commit
to any one of them

1096
00:51:24,620 --> 00:51:27,540
until you see some signs of
life on that core unknown.

1097
00:51:27,540 --> 00:51:30,120
And then if you find signs of
life on one of those projects,

1098
00:51:30,120 --> 00:51:32,380
then you can start to
commit to that project

1099
00:51:32,380 --> 00:51:36,820
after you see some sort
of initial result on it.

1100
00:51:36,820 --> 00:51:39,220
So this is another
brute force approach

1101
00:51:39,220 --> 00:51:41,100
to trying lots of
different things

1102
00:51:41,100 --> 00:51:44,260
so that you can get
through the bad ideas.

1103
00:51:44,260 --> 00:51:47,315
So the question is, in
problem-driven research,

1104
00:51:47,315 --> 00:51:48,940
it's a bit more
comfortable because you

1105
00:51:48,940 --> 00:51:50,120
can try out multiple ideas.

1106
00:51:50,120 --> 00:51:54,980
Are there scenarios where there
isn't like a ton of ideas?

1107
00:51:54,980 --> 00:51:57,580
And yeah, there's
definitely scenarios

1108
00:51:57,580 --> 00:52:01,140
like that where you
may not have tons

1109
00:52:01,140 --> 00:52:03,220
of ideas for solving a problem.

1110
00:52:03,220 --> 00:52:06,993
And I should mention that in
this approach, where you try out

1111
00:52:06,993 --> 00:52:08,660
lots of different
things, you could also

1112
00:52:08,660 --> 00:52:11,380
be trying out multiple
different problems as well.

1113
00:52:11,380 --> 00:52:15,340
And so yeah, you
could be trying out

1114
00:52:15,340 --> 00:52:17,080
like a couple ideas
for one problem,

1115
00:52:17,080 --> 00:52:19,380
try out a couple ideas
for a different problem,

1116
00:52:19,380 --> 00:52:25,130
and then pick your problem based
on what seems to be tractable.

1117
00:52:25,130 --> 00:52:27,350
Because even at the outset,
you don't know how hard

1118
00:52:27,350 --> 00:52:28,610
a problem is going to be.

1119
00:52:28,610 --> 00:52:32,055
And it might be that it's
too hard for the scope

1120
00:52:32,055 --> 00:52:32,930
of a current project.

1121
00:52:32,930 --> 00:52:37,310
Or we don't have good tools or
good ideas for how to solve it.

1122
00:52:37,310 --> 00:52:39,510
Can I give an example of
frontloading a core unknown

1123
00:52:39,510 --> 00:52:41,030
in a project?

1124
00:52:41,030 --> 00:52:42,330
A couple ideas.

1125
00:52:42,330 --> 00:52:44,890
So maybe I'll try to
give a couple examples.

1126
00:52:44,890 --> 00:52:50,170
So one example is I worked
on meta learning in the past.

1127
00:52:50,170 --> 00:52:53,390
And the goal was to
see if you can quickly

1128
00:52:53,390 --> 00:52:58,170
adapt with a very small
amount of data to a problem.

1129
00:52:58,170 --> 00:53:00,270
And the goal was
ultimately to be

1130
00:53:00,270 --> 00:53:02,870
able to get a robot, for
example, to quickly adapt

1131
00:53:02,870 --> 00:53:03,780
to a new task.

1132
00:53:03,780 --> 00:53:06,522

1133
00:53:06,522 --> 00:53:09,830
But actually, designing a meta
learning system on a real robot

1134
00:53:09,830 --> 00:53:12,910
is a ton of work and
would take a lot of work

1135
00:53:12,910 --> 00:53:15,030
to even just do
the coding for it.

1136
00:53:15,030 --> 00:53:20,650
And so what I did is I first
set up an experiment where

1137
00:53:20,650 --> 00:53:24,550
the tasks-- instead of
learning robot tasks,

1138
00:53:24,550 --> 00:53:30,290
the tasks were regressing to
simple 1D sinusoid curves.

1139
00:53:30,290 --> 00:53:31,890
So really, really
simple problem.

1140
00:53:31,890 --> 00:53:35,030
And then just tested, can you
learn with a few data points,

1141
00:53:35,030 --> 00:53:38,850
like five data points,
to regress to the curve?

1142
00:53:38,850 --> 00:53:43,130
And so it was this experiment
that I coded up in a day

1143
00:53:43,130 --> 00:53:45,490
and get some initial
results from.

1144
00:53:45,490 --> 00:53:47,910
And it ended up working
out pretty well.

1145
00:53:47,910 --> 00:53:53,490
And so then that gave me
the signs of life to try it.

1146
00:53:53,490 --> 00:53:58,462
Another example of this,
maybe a more recent example,

1147
00:53:58,462 --> 00:54:00,170
I've been thinking a
little bit about how

1148
00:54:00,170 --> 00:54:02,335
to get robots to follow
different kinds of language

1149
00:54:02,335 --> 00:54:04,210
instructions, including
language instructions

1150
00:54:04,210 --> 00:54:05,650
for different strategies.

1151
00:54:05,650 --> 00:54:08,230
And there's a couple
of risks for this.

1152
00:54:08,230 --> 00:54:10,897
One is getting good
labels for the strategies.

1153
00:54:10,897 --> 00:54:12,730
And the second is
actually getting the robot

1154
00:54:12,730 --> 00:54:14,688
to pay attention to those
different strategies.

1155
00:54:14,688 --> 00:54:18,482

1156
00:54:18,482 --> 00:54:19,940
And that second
one actually seemed

1157
00:54:19,940 --> 00:54:21,727
like the bigger challenge.

1158
00:54:21,727 --> 00:54:24,060
And I took a data set where
it was like a really, really

1159
00:54:24,060 --> 00:54:26,085
simple problem, where
in some of the data,

1160
00:54:26,085 --> 00:54:27,460
the robot used
the right gripper.

1161
00:54:27,460 --> 00:54:29,640
And in some of the data, the
robot used the left gripper.

1162
00:54:29,640 --> 00:54:31,265
And then I trained
a policy just to see

1163
00:54:31,265 --> 00:54:34,920
if it could do that very simple
thing, very easy to evaluate,

1164
00:54:34,920 --> 00:54:37,342
and I could
automatically determine

1165
00:54:37,342 --> 00:54:39,300
if it was using the right
or left from the data

1166
00:54:39,300 --> 00:54:40,600
rather than doing labeling.

1167
00:54:40,600 --> 00:54:43,740
And so that kind of isolated
a core risk of the problem

1168
00:54:43,740 --> 00:54:47,860
before moving on to the broader
problem of getting a robot

1169
00:54:47,860 --> 00:54:51,120
to follow strategies.

1170
00:54:51,120 --> 00:54:53,240
Now, you have a problem.

1171
00:54:53,240 --> 00:54:54,240
You have an idea.

1172
00:54:54,240 --> 00:54:56,003
You want to get things to work.

1173
00:54:56,003 --> 00:54:58,420
One thing that's really hard
in terms of getting something

1174
00:54:58,420 --> 00:55:00,253
to work is if you're
starting with something

1175
00:55:00,253 --> 00:55:01,740
that doesn't work.

1176
00:55:01,740 --> 00:55:04,620
So if you coded up a system
and it's not working,

1177
00:55:04,620 --> 00:55:08,900
there's a million reasons that
could be why it's not working.

1178
00:55:08,900 --> 00:55:11,860
And it's really hard to figure
out which of those things

1179
00:55:11,860 --> 00:55:14,153
might be causing it to not work.

1180
00:55:14,153 --> 00:55:15,320
You can make visualizations.

1181
00:55:15,320 --> 00:55:18,480
There's various tools for
trying to figure that out.

1182
00:55:18,480 --> 00:55:20,860
But it's much easier to start
with something that works

1183
00:55:20,860 --> 00:55:22,680
and then make it
harder than to start

1184
00:55:22,680 --> 00:55:25,520
with something that doesn't
work and try to get it to work.

1185
00:55:25,520 --> 00:55:28,320
And so one thing that's
very useful in research

1186
00:55:28,320 --> 00:55:31,080
is to-- if you're in a scenario
where things aren't working,

1187
00:55:31,080 --> 00:55:32,480
simplify the
problem dramatically

1188
00:55:32,480 --> 00:55:33,980
until something does work.

1189
00:55:33,980 --> 00:55:36,080
And then move it back
to the harder setting

1190
00:55:36,080 --> 00:55:40,320
by, for example, adding more
data, more tasks, whatever.

1191
00:55:40,320 --> 00:55:43,560
And yeah, really useful
debugging strategy.

1192
00:55:43,560 --> 00:55:45,920
And I've seen, yeah,
a lot of projects

1193
00:55:45,920 --> 00:55:47,320
where things aren't working.

1194
00:55:47,320 --> 00:55:50,466
And you just kind of need to
go back to the drawing board.

1195
00:55:50,466 --> 00:55:53,580
Maybe as a very specific
example for this, at one point,

1196
00:55:53,580 --> 00:55:58,240
I was trying to
train robots to be

1197
00:55:58,240 --> 00:56:01,160
able to see a video of
a human doing a task

1198
00:56:01,160 --> 00:56:05,280
and then do the task themselves
with imitation learning.

1199
00:56:05,280 --> 00:56:08,260
Collected a really diverse data
set of robot demonstrations

1200
00:56:08,260 --> 00:56:10,640
and then asked a human
to do what the robot did

1201
00:56:10,640 --> 00:56:11,940
of all sorts of tasks.

1202
00:56:11,940 --> 00:56:15,210
And we just told people
collecting the data,

1203
00:56:15,210 --> 00:56:16,150
do whatever you want.

1204
00:56:16,150 --> 00:56:18,090
Do lots of things.

1205
00:56:18,090 --> 00:56:21,490
Nothing worked when
training on this data.

1206
00:56:21,490 --> 00:56:26,810
Spent like months trying
to get the robot to do it.

1207
00:56:26,810 --> 00:56:30,730
And then what we did is we
simplified to just a single task

1208
00:56:30,730 --> 00:56:31,230
setting.

1209
00:56:31,230 --> 00:56:32,988
Like, can we get the
robot to do one task

1210
00:56:32,988 --> 00:56:35,030
and make sure our imitation
learning stack works?

1211
00:56:35,030 --> 00:56:36,130
It actually didn't work.

1212
00:56:36,130 --> 00:56:39,050
And so we had to debug that,
get it to work on one task,

1213
00:56:39,050 --> 00:56:40,670
then get it to work
on three tasks,

1214
00:56:40,670 --> 00:56:44,050
then get it to work on 10 tasks.

1215
00:56:44,050 --> 00:56:45,930
So yeah, starting
with something that

1216
00:56:45,930 --> 00:56:48,210
works-- really, really helpful.

1217
00:56:48,210 --> 00:56:50,970
Simplifying is really
helpful for that.

1218
00:56:50,970 --> 00:56:54,570
Another thing, it's really
easy to get in your own head

1219
00:56:54,570 --> 00:56:59,190
and just have trouble debugging
something just by yourself.

1220
00:56:59,190 --> 00:57:01,270
And so talking to
friends, colleagues,

1221
00:57:01,270 --> 00:57:03,770
advisors can also
be helpful and help

1222
00:57:03,770 --> 00:57:06,850
you figure out whether it
makes sense to jump up a level

1223
00:57:06,850 --> 00:57:10,550
and reconsider the problem or
keep on tuning hyperparameters

1224
00:57:10,550 --> 00:57:13,050
or try something that
you didn't think of.

1225
00:57:13,050 --> 00:57:16,160
And then also,
revisiting assumptions.

1226
00:57:16,160 --> 00:57:18,060
So you might go into
a project thinking

1227
00:57:18,060 --> 00:57:22,283
that something is a lot easier
than it turned out to be.

1228
00:57:22,283 --> 00:57:24,700
And then you might learn through
the course of the project

1229
00:57:24,700 --> 00:57:27,020
that that thing is
actually really hard.

1230
00:57:27,020 --> 00:57:29,260
And so thinking
through, like, has

1231
00:57:29,260 --> 00:57:31,880
anything changed about my
assumptions from the beginning?

1232
00:57:31,880 --> 00:57:33,960
And then if something
has changed,

1233
00:57:33,960 --> 00:57:35,580
would that change
the way that you're

1234
00:57:35,580 --> 00:57:37,372
going about the project
or even the project

1235
00:57:37,372 --> 00:57:40,260
that you're working
on as a result?

1236
00:57:40,260 --> 00:57:45,760
And then maybe I'll also
talk about a story for this.

1237
00:57:45,760 --> 00:57:49,498
So there's scenarios
where you're trying

1238
00:57:49,498 --> 00:57:50,540
to get something to work.

1239
00:57:50,540 --> 00:57:52,120
And it doesn't
really want to work.

1240
00:57:52,120 --> 00:57:54,300
It's not really easy
to get it to work.

1241
00:57:54,300 --> 00:57:57,780
And if it doesn't really
want to work, then probably

1242
00:57:57,780 --> 00:57:59,495
the idea won't be
very impactful.

1243
00:57:59,495 --> 00:58:01,620
Because if someone else is
trying to get it to work

1244
00:58:01,620 --> 00:58:04,340
and it also doesn't
really work for them,

1245
00:58:04,340 --> 00:58:08,940
then maybe the idea just
wasn't really meant to be.

1246
00:58:08,940 --> 00:58:11,960
And at one point, we
had this project where--

1247
00:58:11,960 --> 00:58:13,885
this is actually a
fairly recent project

1248
00:58:13,885 --> 00:58:15,760
where we were trying to
get a robot to follow

1249
00:58:15,760 --> 00:58:16,968
open-ended language commands.

1250
00:58:16,968 --> 00:58:19,800

1251
00:58:19,800 --> 00:58:22,920
Can you clean up the
trash but not the dishes?

1252
00:58:22,920 --> 00:58:25,440
We also wanted to see if
the robot could handle

1253
00:58:25,440 --> 00:58:27,400
various interventions, like--

1254
00:58:27,400 --> 00:58:29,720
and follow lots of different
prompts, for example.

1255
00:58:29,720 --> 00:58:32,320
Then we kind of got some
initial signs of life on that.

1256
00:58:32,320 --> 00:58:34,195
And we're like, oh,
well, maybe we could also

1257
00:58:34,195 --> 00:58:37,640
get the robot to ask questions
if the prompt is ambiguous.

1258
00:58:37,640 --> 00:58:40,040
And we had all these
kind of cool ideas

1259
00:58:40,040 --> 00:58:43,520
for how we could incorporate the
open-ended language following

1260
00:58:43,520 --> 00:58:46,200
in a robot system.

1261
00:58:46,200 --> 00:58:50,360
And despite having
positive initial signs

1262
00:58:50,360 --> 00:58:53,140
of life, after we
introduced all those ideas,

1263
00:58:53,140 --> 00:58:54,840
things weren't really working.

1264
00:58:54,840 --> 00:58:58,437
And we kind of tried
all sorts of-- we

1265
00:58:58,437 --> 00:59:00,520
spent a couple of months
trying to get it to work.

1266
00:59:00,520 --> 00:59:04,760
And at some point,
we kind of went back

1267
00:59:04,760 --> 00:59:10,370
and thought about, well,
OK, it was working before.

1268
00:59:10,370 --> 00:59:13,033
What parts of this
thing do work?

1269
00:59:13,033 --> 00:59:14,450
And what parts of
these things are

1270
00:59:14,450 --> 00:59:16,850
harder to work with
our current method?

1271
00:59:16,850 --> 00:59:21,010
And then we rescoped the
project to remove the hard parts

1272
00:59:21,010 --> 00:59:22,750
that we didn't know
how to solve yet

1273
00:59:22,750 --> 00:59:26,370
and to only consider
the kind of interactions

1274
00:59:26,370 --> 00:59:28,330
that our method did work with.

1275
00:59:28,330 --> 00:59:33,930
And so it was an example
where we scoped the project

1276
00:59:33,930 --> 00:59:37,110
to the source of things that
the method wanted to work on,

1277
00:59:37,110 --> 00:59:39,410
it worked easily on,
and left the things

1278
00:59:39,410 --> 00:59:41,150
that it didn't handle
for future work.

1279
00:59:41,150 --> 00:59:43,930
We described it as
limitations in the paper.

1280
00:59:43,930 --> 00:59:46,370
And so scoping down
the project, especially

1281
00:59:46,370 --> 00:59:50,290
if you scoped it up a
little bit too eagerly,

1282
00:59:50,290 --> 00:59:52,520
can also be a useful
strategy in research.

1283
00:59:52,520 --> 00:59:55,690

1284
00:59:55,690 --> 00:59:58,450
Last thing, if you're in a
scenario where things really

1285
00:59:58,450 --> 01:00:00,690
aren't working, you might
decide whether or not

1286
01:00:00,690 --> 01:00:02,170
you should pivot
or whether or not

1287
01:00:02,170 --> 01:00:05,450
you should start working
on a different project.

1288
01:00:05,450 --> 01:00:07,170
Oftentimes, you'll
consider this later

1289
01:00:07,170 --> 01:00:10,350
than you should be considering
this because you really

1290
01:00:10,350 --> 01:00:12,310
want to get something to work.

1291
01:00:12,310 --> 01:00:13,950
There's also this
sunk cost fallacy,

1292
01:00:13,950 --> 01:00:17,930
where if you spent a lot
of time on something, then

1293
01:00:17,930 --> 01:00:19,732
it's very natural
for humans to think

1294
01:00:19,732 --> 01:00:21,690
that you'll spend less
time on it in the future

1295
01:00:21,690 --> 01:00:23,870
because you spend a lot
of time on it in the past.

1296
01:00:23,870 --> 01:00:26,030
This is a fallacy,
not always true.

1297
01:00:26,030 --> 01:00:29,550
And you might actually not
have made as much progress

1298
01:00:29,550 --> 01:00:33,350
on the problem than the
time that you spent on

1299
01:00:33,350 --> 01:00:34,340
it would suggest.

1300
01:00:34,340 --> 01:00:37,110

1301
01:00:37,110 --> 01:00:40,310
Now, deciding when to pivot is
really hard because you're often

1302
01:00:40,310 --> 01:00:41,750
thinking about,
should I continue

1303
01:00:41,750 --> 01:00:43,130
working on my current project?

1304
01:00:43,130 --> 01:00:46,470
Or should I switch to
starting a new project?

1305
01:00:46,470 --> 01:00:49,990
And this option of
switching to a new project

1306
01:00:49,990 --> 01:00:51,620
is really nebulous.

1307
01:00:51,620 --> 01:00:53,370
This could mean a lot
of different things.

1308
01:00:53,370 --> 01:00:55,578
There's a lot of different
things you might be doing.

1309
01:00:55,578 --> 01:00:57,250
And so it's really
hard to think about,

1310
01:00:57,250 --> 01:00:59,290
should I be doing what
I'm currently doing?

1311
01:00:59,290 --> 01:01:00,630
Or should I do something else?

1312
01:01:00,630 --> 01:01:04,670
Because something else
could be anything.

1313
01:01:04,670 --> 01:01:07,440
And so one kind of mental
trick for thinking about

1314
01:01:07,440 --> 01:01:09,840
this sort of decision
about whether to pivot

1315
01:01:09,840 --> 01:01:12,373
is to change this
decision and actually

1316
01:01:12,373 --> 01:01:14,040
spend time thinking
about other projects

1317
01:01:14,040 --> 01:01:16,960
that you would do and
then try to formulate

1318
01:01:16,960 --> 01:01:18,760
the decision as
continuing working

1319
01:01:18,760 --> 01:01:21,340
on the current project
versus working on project B

1320
01:01:21,340 --> 01:01:24,240
or working on project
C and so forth.

1321
01:01:24,240 --> 01:01:27,600
And this decision is much
more concrete, a lot more

1322
01:01:27,600 --> 01:01:31,120
straightforward to think about,
less anxiety inducing, and so

1323
01:01:31,120 --> 01:01:33,000
forth.

1324
01:01:33,000 --> 01:01:37,240
So the question is, when
research fields are moving

1325
01:01:37,240 --> 01:01:42,560
really quickly, you might be
worried that another group will

1326
01:01:42,560 --> 01:01:45,960
do the same thing and
then try to spend less

1327
01:01:45,960 --> 01:01:48,840
time on the evaluation just to
try to get the paper out faster.

1328
01:01:48,840 --> 01:01:51,160
Is that a trend?

1329
01:01:51,160 --> 01:01:53,440
What do I think about that?

1330
01:01:53,440 --> 01:02:01,960
So there's definitely
papers that have spent--

1331
01:02:01,960 --> 01:02:04,540
that should be more thorough
with the evaluation.

1332
01:02:04,540 --> 01:02:05,660
That's been true.

1333
01:02:05,660 --> 01:02:06,480
That's true now.

1334
01:02:06,480 --> 01:02:09,740
That's also been
true in the past.

1335
01:02:09,740 --> 01:02:15,420
I don't know if it's been
getting worse per se.

1336
01:02:15,420 --> 01:02:19,960
I think that if there
is a feeling to do that,

1337
01:02:19,960 --> 01:02:22,380
I think you really want to be
confident about the results

1338
01:02:22,380 --> 01:02:23,900
that you're putting out.

1339
01:02:23,900 --> 01:02:27,660
And I think that if you're
worried about being scooped

1340
01:02:27,660 --> 01:02:35,630
or another group
doing something,

1341
01:02:35,630 --> 01:02:40,920
there is an advantage to having
done some sort of idea first.

1342
01:02:40,920 --> 01:02:43,900
But there's also a lot of
advantage to executing really

1343
01:02:43,900 --> 01:02:45,780
well on a project.

1344
01:02:45,780 --> 01:02:48,580
And sometimes, the projects
that are executed on better

1345
01:02:48,580 --> 01:02:50,460
are more impactful
than the projects

1346
01:02:50,460 --> 01:02:53,300
that were like first
at a particular idea.

1347
01:02:53,300 --> 01:02:59,700
And so I guess my
perspective is to still put

1348
01:02:59,700 --> 01:03:03,410
a fair amount of focus on
execution and evaluation.

1349
01:03:03,410 --> 01:03:05,250
Now, there's many things
you could evaluate.

1350
01:03:05,250 --> 01:03:07,510
You could run your
experiments on 20 data sets.

1351
01:03:07,510 --> 01:03:10,870
And so there's a limit
to what you want to do.

1352
01:03:10,870 --> 01:03:14,070
So I think there's a balance.

1353
01:03:14,070 --> 01:03:19,070
And certainly, there are some
parts of the research field

1354
01:03:19,070 --> 01:03:20,130
that move fast.

1355
01:03:20,130 --> 01:03:22,590
One thing that I've
liked about real robots

1356
01:03:22,590 --> 01:03:26,350
is that a small enough people
work with like real hardware

1357
01:03:26,350 --> 01:03:29,235
that you have to worry-- you
can worry less about that

1358
01:03:29,235 --> 01:03:31,110
in many ways, especially
if you execute on it

1359
01:03:31,110 --> 01:03:32,985
and actually show the
results on real robots.

1360
01:03:32,985 --> 01:03:35,890

1361
01:03:35,890 --> 01:03:36,855
But there's a balance.

1362
01:03:36,855 --> 01:03:39,230
And it's also good to work on
things like language models

1363
01:03:39,230 --> 01:03:41,340
and things that are hot too.

1364
01:03:41,340 --> 01:03:42,340
Yeah, there's a balance.

1365
01:03:42,340 --> 01:03:46,790

1366
01:03:46,790 --> 01:03:48,830
So how to share your research?

1367
01:03:48,830 --> 01:03:52,730
I talked about why is
this really important.

1368
01:03:52,730 --> 01:03:56,350
So if you think about what
is the output or the outcome

1369
01:03:56,350 --> 01:03:59,390
of research, the
output of research

1370
01:03:59,390 --> 01:04:03,370
is essentially ideas, knowledge,
things that you learned

1371
01:04:03,370 --> 01:04:04,930
from doing the research.

1372
01:04:04,930 --> 01:04:06,550
It's not a product.

1373
01:04:06,550 --> 01:04:07,710
It's not a service.

1374
01:04:07,710 --> 01:04:08,790
It's really knowledge.

1375
01:04:08,790 --> 01:04:10,810
It's things that you learn.

1376
01:04:10,810 --> 01:04:17,183
And if it were a product,
then the high impact

1377
01:04:17,183 --> 01:04:18,850
would mean that lots
of people are using

1378
01:04:18,850 --> 01:04:20,690
the product for good things.

1379
01:04:20,690 --> 01:04:22,410
But since it's more
like knowledge,

1380
01:04:22,410 --> 01:04:26,170
if no one knows about what you
learned, then there was really

1381
01:04:26,170 --> 01:04:31,028
no impact or no output as a
result. And even for companies

1382
01:04:31,028 --> 01:04:32,570
that have products
and services, they

1383
01:04:32,570 --> 01:04:34,112
have massive marketing
efforts to try

1384
01:04:34,112 --> 01:04:35,930
to get those products out.

1385
01:04:35,930 --> 01:04:38,750
And so for your work to have
impact, you need to share it.

1386
01:04:38,750 --> 01:04:40,450
And you need people
to know about it.

1387
01:04:40,450 --> 01:04:43,210
Otherwise, it won't
have had impact.

1388
01:04:43,210 --> 01:04:48,110
Now, there can be reasons to not
be excited about sharing work.

1389
01:04:48,110 --> 01:04:50,570
You can say maybe it
feels like self-promotion.

1390
01:04:50,570 --> 01:04:53,010
Maybe the idea didn't
work that well so I'm not

1391
01:04:53,010 --> 01:04:54,510
that excited about sharing it.

1392
01:04:54,510 --> 01:04:56,610
Maybe it seems all obvious.

1393
01:04:56,610 --> 01:04:58,820
All of it seems
obvious to you now.

1394
01:04:58,820 --> 01:05:00,580
And so why do you
need to share it?

1395
01:05:00,580 --> 01:05:02,962
But even if it feels
like self-promotion,

1396
01:05:02,962 --> 01:05:03,920
you're teaching people.

1397
01:05:03,920 --> 01:05:05,620
You're sharing
cool new findings.

1398
01:05:05,620 --> 01:05:08,078
And you can think about it as
more actually doing a service

1399
01:05:08,078 --> 01:05:11,620
to them to teach them something
new and useful and interesting.

1400
01:05:11,620 --> 01:05:13,240
Even if it didn't
work that well,

1401
01:05:13,240 --> 01:05:15,740
it's really useful to share
because other people might

1402
01:05:15,740 --> 01:05:17,540
think of that idea
and might want

1403
01:05:17,540 --> 01:05:19,080
to know how well did it work.

1404
01:05:19,080 --> 01:05:21,200
And did it work in
certain circumstances

1405
01:05:21,200 --> 01:05:24,020
more better than
other circumstances?

1406
01:05:24,020 --> 01:05:25,640
And then, yeah, after
enough research,

1407
01:05:25,640 --> 01:05:27,838
you're going to know a lot
more than other people.

1408
01:05:27,838 --> 01:05:30,380
There will be things that seem
really obvious to you that are

1409
01:05:30,380 --> 01:05:32,020
not obvious to other people.

1410
01:05:32,020 --> 01:05:34,580
And it's good to still
share those things

1411
01:05:34,580 --> 01:05:38,460
and think about what actually
other people don't know

1412
01:05:38,460 --> 01:05:41,375
so that you can share
the things that you

1413
01:05:41,375 --> 01:05:43,500
have learned, especially
the most important things,

1414
01:05:43,500 --> 01:05:45,860
with them.

1415
01:05:45,860 --> 01:05:50,580
Now, I could probably
give an entire lecture

1416
01:05:50,580 --> 01:05:54,220
on presenting research and
writing and presentations and so

1417
01:05:54,220 --> 01:05:55,220
forth.

1418
01:05:55,220 --> 01:05:56,960
But yeah, having
really clear writing,

1419
01:05:56,960 --> 01:05:59,760
really clear visuals,
really clear presentations.

1420
01:05:59,760 --> 01:06:02,120
Communicating clearly is
really, really important.

1421
01:06:02,120 --> 01:06:03,980
If they don't understand
what you're saying,

1422
01:06:03,980 --> 01:06:06,407
then they won't
understand the knowledge.

1423
01:06:06,407 --> 01:06:08,240
It's important to think
about their audience

1424
01:06:08,240 --> 01:06:11,040
and how they will interpret
what you say, what they know.

1425
01:06:11,040 --> 01:06:12,760
When in doubt, assume
that your audience

1426
01:06:12,760 --> 01:06:15,182
knows less rather than more.

1427
01:06:15,182 --> 01:06:17,140
This can actually be a
little bit tricky to do.

1428
01:06:17,140 --> 01:06:19,380
It's good to avoid
jargon if possible.

1429
01:06:19,380 --> 01:06:20,720
They might know the jargon.

1430
01:06:20,720 --> 01:06:22,680
But if there's ways
to avoid it, then it

1431
01:06:22,680 --> 01:06:24,600
makes it more accessible.

1432
01:06:24,600 --> 01:06:26,640
And even if they do
know a lot of what

1433
01:06:26,640 --> 01:06:28,280
you're going to
say, a lot of people

1434
01:06:28,280 --> 01:06:30,480
still appreciate
refreshers, especially

1435
01:06:30,480 --> 01:06:32,225
if those refreshers are clear.

1436
01:06:32,225 --> 01:06:34,600
And it might actually cause
them to think about something

1437
01:06:34,600 --> 01:06:37,215
that they know in a new way.

1438
01:06:37,215 --> 01:06:38,840
Other ways to get
better at this-- just

1439
01:06:38,840 --> 01:06:41,240
practicing-- practicing
writing, practicing, giving

1440
01:06:41,240 --> 01:06:42,560
presentations.

1441
01:06:42,560 --> 01:06:43,620
You can practice alone.

1442
01:06:43,620 --> 01:06:47,040
You can practice with other
people if it's a presentation.

1443
01:06:47,040 --> 01:06:48,940
And then just try
to get feedback.

1444
01:06:48,940 --> 01:06:50,560
Try to get honest
feedback from people

1445
01:06:50,560 --> 01:06:51,810
and improve with the feedback.

1446
01:06:51,810 --> 01:06:54,280

1447
01:06:54,280 --> 01:06:57,890
Now, when trying
to write a paper

1448
01:06:57,890 --> 01:07:00,642
or when trying to create
a slide deck or something,

1449
01:07:00,642 --> 01:07:02,350
it's easy to get a
little bit overwhelmed

1450
01:07:02,350 --> 01:07:04,930
by how much you
have to do and where

1451
01:07:04,930 --> 01:07:06,850
even to get started on that.

1452
01:07:06,850 --> 01:07:09,050
I find it really helpful
to break down the task.

1453
01:07:09,050 --> 01:07:12,210
And even on a pen and paper,
just write down some ideas

1454
01:07:12,210 --> 01:07:14,170
for how I want to do it.

1455
01:07:14,170 --> 01:07:16,050
Maybe write down an outline.

1456
01:07:16,050 --> 01:07:18,330
My recommendation would be
to think through your ideas

1457
01:07:18,330 --> 01:07:20,983
for what you want to communicate
before asking your friend,

1458
01:07:20,983 --> 01:07:22,400
before asking your
friend ChatGPT.

1459
01:07:22,400 --> 01:07:25,090

1460
01:07:25,090 --> 01:07:27,130
And then a couple
miscellaneous things.

1461
01:07:27,130 --> 01:07:28,590
First is mentorship.

1462
01:07:28,590 --> 01:07:31,630
I think mentorship can play
a huge role in research.

1463
01:07:31,630 --> 01:07:35,290
And if you have it, don't
be afraid to lean on it.

1464
01:07:35,290 --> 01:07:38,350
In my first research
paper in my PhD,

1465
01:07:38,350 --> 01:07:42,670
I like hardly wrote
any of the paper.

1466
01:07:42,670 --> 01:07:44,277
And I talked to
actually my co-author.

1467
01:07:44,277 --> 01:07:46,610
And he told me that he actually
even rewrote the section

1468
01:07:46,610 --> 01:07:49,050
that I wrote.

1469
01:07:49,050 --> 01:07:51,730
And so it's OK to not
know everything and not

1470
01:07:51,730 --> 01:07:54,610
know how to do every part
of the research process.

1471
01:07:54,610 --> 01:07:56,710
And lean on other
people for the things

1472
01:07:56,710 --> 01:07:58,750
that you don't
know how to do yet.

1473
01:07:58,750 --> 01:08:02,110
Generally, I've seen
students and myself

1474
01:08:02,110 --> 01:08:04,830
do most successful when
they're learning more gradually

1475
01:08:04,830 --> 01:08:07,375
and when they're not trying to
take on everything themselves

1476
01:08:07,375 --> 01:08:09,750
because doing novel research
and writing a paper about it

1477
01:08:09,750 --> 01:08:12,390
and presenting it-- like,
picking a problem, all of that

1478
01:08:12,390 --> 01:08:14,950
is a really, really
huge undertaking.

1479
01:08:14,950 --> 01:08:17,229
And it's useful to
lean on other people

1480
01:08:17,229 --> 01:08:18,970
as you're trying to
learn how to do that.

1481
01:08:18,970 --> 01:08:22,229

1482
01:08:22,229 --> 01:08:23,490
And then confidence.

1483
01:08:23,490 --> 01:08:27,830
So there's a lot of reasons to
lack confidence in research.

1484
01:08:27,830 --> 01:08:30,270
No one knows how to
do research right now.

1485
01:08:30,270 --> 01:08:32,910
No one knows what research is
going to be most impactful.

1486
01:08:32,910 --> 01:08:35,270
If they did, it
wouldn't be research.

1487
01:08:35,270 --> 01:08:36,370
Many ideas don't work.

1488
01:08:36,370 --> 01:08:38,212
Many papers get rejected.

1489
01:08:38,212 --> 01:08:39,670
Many researchers
have been thinking

1490
01:08:39,670 --> 01:08:42,029
about a domain for many years.

1491
01:08:42,029 --> 01:08:44,830
And they will be smarter
than you in that domain

1492
01:08:44,830 --> 01:08:47,990
because they've been thinking
about it for many years.

1493
01:08:47,990 --> 01:08:51,560
And so it's easy to think that
you know very little, that you

1494
01:08:51,560 --> 01:08:54,680
have been rejected many times,
that a lot of your ideas

1495
01:08:54,680 --> 01:08:58,010
